window.SLIDES = [{"n": 1, "part": 1, "title": "Putting the System Together", "paras": ["Good evening, everyone. Let's wait a couple of minutes, so more people can join (joyn). OK, let's start. Welcome to our (aw·r) Computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) Vision (vi·zhn) class. Today we start Module (maa·jool) four (4), which, as you know, is the last module (maa·jool) of the course. This week, the pieces you built over three (3) weeks become a complete system: one that another person can use, without you telling them what to do or what to press. Today, in Class four point one (4.1), we put it together. On Wednesday (wenz·day), in Class four point two (4.2), we show it, and look at where it could go. And look at the photo on the cover (kuh·vr). A crew (kroo), filling a pothole (paat·howl). In the end, that's who a system like ours is for. Before we start, one idea for this week. For three (3) weeks we worked for ourselves. The detector (duh·tek·tr) had to work on our (aw·r) computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr), with our (aw·r) folders and our (aw·r) photos (fow·towz). This week we change the point of view. Now we think about another person: someone who opens the system for the first time, and knows nothing (nuh·thuhng) about how we built it. Everything we'll see today, the detection (duh·tek·shn) page, the connected pages, the hook and the README (reed·mee), comes from that one question (kwes·chn): what does that person need to use the system without help?"]}, {"n": 2, "part": 1, "title": "Last Week: Your Detector Learned", "paras": ["Before we start, a quick look back at last week.", "1. First, the training page. You chose which photos (fow·towz) to train on, you said why, and your detector (duh·tek·tr) learned (lurnd) your objects (aab·jekts). That page was the first time training left the notebook. You didn't run cells by hand. You pressed a button, and the page saved the run with its reason. We'll see that reason again today, because (buh·kuhz) it travels with the model all the way to the detection (duh·tek·shn) page.", "2. Second, the confidence (kaan·fuh·duhns) threshold (thresh·howld). Every move adds something and takes something away. And you chose one, thinking about who pays for each mistake. And that decision (duh·si·zhn) wasn't made by the model. The model gives (givz) each box (baaks) a score, and you decided (duh·sai·duhd) from which score you believe (buh·leev) it. Today that decision (duh·si·zhn) becomes the value the page starts with.", "3. Third (thurd), you looked at the images (i·muh·juhz). You didn't stop at the number. You looked for where your detector (duh·tek·tr) gets it wrong (rong). That's what separates someone who trains a model from someone who understands it. The mAP (em·ay·pee) number tells you how much. The images (i·muh·juhz) tell you where, and why. And those images (i·muh·juhz) will be the base of the most honest (aa·nuhst) section of the README (reed·mee): what doesn't work.", "4. Today, the last step. All of that becomes one system that another person can run. And there are no new neural (nur·uhl) network concepts this week. This week is about something that, in industry (in·duh·stree), matters (ma·drz) as much as the model: can another person use it, and trust it? And for this week you need three (3) things ready from the weeks before: your converted dataset (day·tuh·set), at least one finished run with its best.pt, and your chosen threshold (thresh·howld). If any of the three is missing (mi·suhng), that's the first step, before you start the new page."]}, {"n": 3, "part": 1, "title": "Where We Are", "paras": ["Here's where we are on the course map (em·ay·pee). Classical vision (vi·zhn), CNNs and our (aw·r) project, training: done. Now, Module (maa·jool) four (4): the full system.", "1. Today, in Class four point one (4.1): the detection (duh·tek·shn) page, connecting the three (3) pages, hooks, and the README (reed·mee). The order matters (ma·drz). First the page, because (buh·kuhz) without it there's no system. Then the connections, because (buh·kuhz) three (3) separate (seh·pruht) pages aren't a system. Then the hook, which protects the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee). And last the README (reed·mee), which is what lets another person get here without you.", "2. On Wednesday (wenz·day), in Class four point two (4.2): how to show your system in three (3) minutes, what it would take to use it for real, what these networks can do besides detecting, and the close of the course. The second class is calmer. There's no new code. It's about showing what you built, and thinking about what would happen if a real city wanted to use it. And we close with what these same networks do in other fields."]}, {"n": 4, "part": 1, "title": "When Is a Project a System?", "paras": ["But first, a question (kwes·chn). When does a project stop being a project, and become a system? In this course, we'll use three (3) signs.", "1. First, it runs on another computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr), not only on yours. If it only works on your laptop, it isn't a system yet. Why does that happen? Because (buh·kuhz) your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) has things nobody else has: a version of Python (pai·thaan), some folders, a dataset (day·tuh·set) you already downloaded. You take all of that for granted. The other person doesn't have it. And you can see this easily in the course. A classmate (klas·mayt) clones your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), and the first thing that fails is almost (aal·mowst) never the detector (duh·tek·tr). It's the installation: a different Python (pai·thaan) version, a missing (mi·suhng) package, or a folder that doesn't exist.", "2. Second, another person can follow it, without you next to them explaining. That's the job of a good README (reed·mee), and we'll see it today. And why is that so hard? Because (buh·kuhz) you already know everything. You know which folder to open, which command goes first, and which error you can ignore. The other person knows none of that. A good README (reed·mee) is one that works for someone who knows nothing (nuh·thuhng).", "3. And third (thurd), it says what it can't do, before someone finds (faindz) out the hard way. These three (3) signs are, in the end, what I'll (ail) ask you for in Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point one (4.1). Think about the crew (kroo) on the cover (kuh·vr). If the system says a photo has no potholes (paat·howlz), they don't send the truck. If it misses small potholes (paat·howlz) and nobody told them, they made that decision (duh·si·zhn) with half the information (in·fr·may·shn). And all three (3) signs are about another person, not about the model. An excellent (ek·suh·luhnt) model that nobody else can run meets none of them."]}, {"n": 5, "part": 1, "title": "The Reference System, End to End", "paras": ["Here's the reference system, the pothole (paat·howl) one, complete. Claude (klaad) Code built this app on the course test project, from the same specifications (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shnz) you have.", "1. First, the data page, from Module (maa·jool) two (2). What it trains on. This page is the one from Module (maa·jool) two (2). It shows how many photos (fow·towz) there are, how many boxes (baak·suhz) per class, and a few (fyoo) examples with their (dhehr) boxes (baak·suhz). For someone new, that's the first answer to the question (kwes·chn): what did this system learn from?", "2. Second, the training page, from Module (maa·jool) three (3). The run, and why it was trained (traynd) that way. Here the person sees the finished run, its curves (kurvz), and the reason. It's the page from Module (maa·jool) three (3). And the person doesn't have to train anything to use the system. The run is already done and saved.", "3. And third (thurd), the new page: detection (duh·tek·shn) and results. I upload a photo, and the boxes (baak·suhz) appear. It's the same kind of test photo we used last week. But now it isn't in a notebook. It's on a page that anyone can use with a mouse. And the page also lets you pick one of the test photos (fow·towz), without uploading anything. That's useful for someone who just wants to see whether (weh·thr) the system works, before trying their (dhehr) own photos (fow·towz).", "4. One app, three (3) pages. This week, you build the third (thurd) one. And here's the thing. You already have the first two (2) pages. We won't rebuild them. This week we only add the third (thurd) one, and make the three (3) work together."]}, {"n": 6, "part": 1, "title": "The Detection Page", "paras": ["Let's start with the new page: detection (duh·tek·shn). What has to happen between the model you trained (traynd) and a box (baaks) on a new photo?"]}, {"n": 7, "part": 1, "title": "From a Run to a Box", "paras": ["Four (4) steps.", "1. First, you choose a run: the best.pt file that came (kaym) out of page two (2). Second, the model runs once on the photo, and returns every box (baaks), even the ones with almost (aal·mowst) zero confidence (kaan·fuh·duhns). Third (thurd), we keep only the ones above (uh·buhv) the threshold (thresh·howld). Fourth, we draw them, count them, and write a sentence. Wait: why return even the boxes (baak·suhz) with almost (aal·mowst) zero confidence (kaan·fuh·duhns)? Because (buh·kuhz) that keeps the threshold (thresh·howld) decision (duh·si·zhn) outside the model, in your hands. The model says everything it sees. You decide (duh·side) what to show. In the code, that almost-zero confidence (kaan·fuh·duhns) is passed once, when the model is called. That's why the model returns many boxes (baak·suhz), sometimes dozens, on a single photo. In one of our (aw·r) real tests it returned more than fifty (50). Then the threshold (thresh·howld) decides which ones are shown.", "2. And here's the key idea. The slow part, running the model, happens (ha·puhnz) only once. Moving the threshold (thresh·howld) just filters what's already there. That's why the page answers instantly when you move the slider. On our (aw·r) Mac, running the model takes a fraction of a second, and filtering takes almost (aal·mowst) nothing (nuh·thuhng). With big photos (fow·towz) or a heavier model, that difference (di·fruhns) is much bigger (bi·gr). Think about what would happen if it weren't like this. Every time someone moved the slider, the page would have to run the model again. The person would move the slider, wait, move it again, and wait again. With one photo you barely (behr·lee) feel it. With many photos (fow·towz), the page gets slow, and nobody wants to use it. Keeping the model apart from the filter is what makes the page feel instant."]}, {"n": 8, "part": 1, "title": "Run Once, Filter Many Times", "paras": ["Here's what that looks like on a real test photo.", "1. First, a low (low) threshold (thresh·howld). Lots of boxes (baak·suhz), and some of them are doubtful. Look closely (klows·lee): some sit on water stains, or on the edge of the road. Those are the ones the model isn't sure about. And this photo is good for the example because (buh·kuhz) it has many potholes (paat·howlz). On a photo with one pothole (paat·howl), you barely (behr·lee) see the difference (di·fruhns) between thresholds. To see the effect of the threshold (thresh·howld), it's better to try a hard photo.", "2. Second, the threshold (thresh·howld) I chose in Module (maa·jool) three (3). Fewer (fyoo·wr) boxes (baak·suhz). Here the most doubtful boxes (baak·suhz) are gone, the ones on the water stains. Most of the ones left are real potholes (paat·howlz). This is the point I chose in Module (maa·jool) three (3), looking at the curve (kurv) and thinking about the crew (kroo).", "3. Third (thurd), a higher threshold (thresh·howld). Only the boxes (baak·suhz) the model is surest about. But real potholes (paat·howlz) disappeared too. That's the price of asking for more confidence (kaan·fuh·duhns): fewer (fyoo·wr) false (fols) alarms, more missed (mist) potholes (paat·howlz). So why not always (aal·wayz) use the highest threshold (thresh·howld)? Because (buh·kuhz) the crew (kroo) doesn't only want safe boxes (baak·suhz). They want to find the potholes (paat·howlz). A system that's never wrong (rong) because (buh·kuhz) it almost (aal·mowst) never says anything isn't useful to anybody either.", "4. And here's the point. Same photo. Same model. One single run. The only thing that changed (chaynjd) was the threshold (thresh·howld). Remember who pays for each mistake? This page lets you see that decision (duh·si·zhn) on any photo."]}, {"n": 9, "part": 1, "title": "RGB and BGR, Again", "paras": ["Now, a technical (tek·nuh·kl) detail that can cost you a whole afternoon if nobody tells you.", "1. Remember Module (maa·jool) one (1)? OpenCV reads photos (fow·towz) as BGR, with the color channels reversed. On the page, the photo the user uploads arrives as RGB. And Ultralytics (uhl·truh·li·tiks) reads NumPy images (i·muh·juhz) as BGR. So if you give it RGB, red becomes blue, and the model sees a different photo. How bad is that? The pothole (paat·howl) is still (stil) there, but the colors change. A brown puddle turns bluish, and the model never saw a pothole (paat·howl) that color. It may find fewer (fyoo·wr), or find strange (straynj) things. And the code gives (givz) no error at all. That's the danger: it fails silently. And this mistake shows up mostly in systems that mix libraries. Gradio (gray·dee·ow) gives (givz) you RGB, OpenCV works in BGR, and each library (lai·breh·ree) assumes its own order. Every time an image goes from one library (lai·breh·ree) to another, it's worth (wurth) asking which order the colors are in.", "2. That's why, inside the function predict, the photo is converted to BGR before it reaches (ree·chuhz) the model. One single line: the one in red. And there's a detail in that line. The conversion (kuhn·vur·zhn) creates a new image. The photo the person uploaded isn't touched. That matters (ma·drz), because (buh·kuhz) the same photo is used later to draw the boxes (baak·suhz), and there it has to be in RGB, with its normal colors.", "3. And we don't trust our (aw·r) memory. One of the tests gives (givz) the function a fake model, and checks that the image arrives in BGR. Forget the line, and the test fails. This is an idea that will help you outside this course. When a mistake gives (givz) no message at all, the only way to catch it is with a test that looks for it on purpose (pur·puhs). If not, the user finds (faindz) it, and almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) too late."]}, {"n": 10, "part": 1, "title": "Your Threshold Becomes the Default", "paras": ["Remember the threshold (thresh·howld) you chose in Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) three point two (3.2)?", "1. That decision (duh·si·zhn) now lives in project.yaml, on a line called threshold (thresh·howld). In the reference system it's zero point two five (0.25). It's a simple text file, one anyone can open and read. It isn't hidden in the code. If someone wants to know which threshold (thresh·howld) the system works with, they find it there, on one line.", "2. And the page starts the slider right there. Think about a user who doesn't know your project. Which threshold (thresh·howld) should they use? They have no idea. So the page starts with your answer: the best decision (duh·si·zhn) you made by looking at the data. And if someone wants a different threshold (thresh·howld), they can move it on the page. The value in project.yaml doesn't lock anybody in. It's just the starting point: the one you recommend.", "3. Your decision (duh·si·zhn), written once, used by the whole system. And CLAUDE.md has a rule (rool): Claude (klaad) never changes it. And why that rule (rool)? Because (buh·kuhz) the threshold (thresh·howld) isn't a technical (tek·nuh·kl) detail. It's a decision (duh·si·zhn) about who pays for each mistake, and that decision (duh·si·zhn) is yours. If Claude (klaad) changed (chaynjd) it to make a test pass, or to make the image look better, it would be making a decision (duh·si·zhn) that isn't its to make."]}, {"n": 11, "part": 1, "title": "Reading Your Curve Before You Trust the Default", "paras": ["Before you trust that default value, it's worth (wurth) looking again at where it came (kaym) from.", "1. This is the curve (kurv) from Module (maa·jool) three (3). Each point is a different threshold (thresh·howld), and for each one we see what happens (ha·puhnz) to precision (pruh·si·zhn) and recall. You don't need to read the numbers (nuhm·brz). What matters (ma·drz) is the shape. When the threshold (thresh·howld) goes up, precision (pruh·si·zhn) goes up and recall goes down. You can't have both at the top. And this curve (kurv) isn't from just any model. It's from the detector (duh·tek·tr) we trained (traynd) in this course, on test photos (fow·towz) it never saw during training.", "2. Left of your threshold (thresh·howld), the system shows more boxes (baak·suhz). It finds (faindz) more potholes (paat·howlz), but also more false (fols) alarms. For a crew (kroo), that means driving out to check places where there's nothing (nuh·thuhng). And this point on the curve (kurv) is where your threshold (thresh·howld) is, marked with a line. On each side of that line, the system behaves differently. That line is your decision (duh·si·zhn).", "3. Right of it, the system is pickier. Almost (aal·mowst) every box (baaks) is a real pothole (paat·howl), but more of them slip through (throo). For the crew (kroo), that means potholes (paat·howlz) nobody fixes, because (buh·kuhz) the system never showed them.", "4. And your point is the trade you chose for your user. There's no right point for everyone. There's a point that makes sense for one person, and one decision (duh·si·zhn). That's why, in the README (reed·mee), writing the number isn't enough (uh·nuhf). Write why, in one sentence. That way, whoever uses the system knows what kind of mistake to expect. For example: I chose a low (low) threshold (thresh·howld) because (buh·kuhz) I'd rather have the crew (kroo) check a false (fols) alarm than leave a pothole (paat·howl) unchecked. With that sentence, anyone understands the decision (duh·si·zhn)."]}, {"n": 12, "part": 1, "title": "The Specification: docs/detect_spec.md", "paras": ["As in every module (maa·jool), it all starts with a specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn): docs/detect_spec.md.", "1. Here are the functions. list_runs finds (faindz) the finished runs, with their (dhehr) mAP (em·ay·pee) fifty (50) and their (dhehr) reason. predict runs the model once. keep filters by the threshold (thresh·howld). count_by_label and describe make the table and the sentence. draw draws (draaz) the boxes (baak·suhz). And default_conf and system_check read your threshold (thresh·howld) and check which parts of the system are ready. That last one, system_check, is what lets the app tell you what's missing (mi·suhng). We'll see it soon. Each function does one single thing. One lists the runs, one runs the model, one filters, one draws (draaz). That isn't by chance. When each function does one thing, you can test it alone, and when something fails, you know exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) where to look. And the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) also says what each function returns, and in what shape. For example, that each prediction (pruh·dik·shn) has a box (baaks), a score and a label. That's what lets the tests check the result without any doubt.", "2. Ten (10) functions, ten (10) tests. And the tests use a fake model, so you don't train anything to check the code. Why fake? Because (buh·kuhz) a test has to be fast, and give the same result every time. A real model is slow, and it changes every time you retrain it. A fake model always (aal·wayz) answers the same way. So if a test fails, the problem is in your code. And in the tests folder you can open the file and read each test. They're short, and reading them is a good way to understand what the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) expects from each function."]}, {"n": 13, "part": 1, "title": "The Ten Tests in tests/test_detect.py", "paras": ["So what exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) do those ten (10) tests check? It's worth (wurth) seeing them one by one, because (buh·kuhz) each one protects something a user would see.", "1. The first two (2) check the list of runs. Only finished runs, sorted, with their (dhehr) mAP (em·ay·pee) and their (dhehr) reason. And if the folder doesn't exist, the list comes out empty, and nothing (nuh·thuhng) crashes. Then one checks that the predictions (pruh·dik·shnz) come out sorted, highest score first. The fourth is the one we saw a moment ago: the model runs once, it receives BGR, and the original photo doesn't change. Then come the filter, the count per class, and the sentence under the photo. Look at the count. A class with no boxes (baak·suhz) has to appear with zero, not disappear from the table. Then, the drawing makes a new image without touching the original. And last, the threshold (thresh·howld) is read from project.yaml, and the system check says what's ready and what's missing (mi·suhng). And many tests check empty cases: no runs, no boxes (baak·suhz), no class found (faund). Those cases are the ones that fail most in a real system, because (buh·kuhz) nobody tests them by hand.", "2. And they all use a fake model. That's why they run in seconds. They don't train anything, and they don't download (daun·lowd) anything. If one fails, the problem is in the code, not in the data or the model. And whenever you change something in detect.py, run these tests before you go on. It's the fastest way to know whether (weh·thr) you broke something. And if you ever add a new function, add its test too. That way the next person who touches the code, or you yourself a month from now, knows nothing (nuh·thuhng) was broken. And if a test fails and you don't understand why, read the test first, not the code. The test says exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) what was expected."]}, {"n": 14, "part": 1, "title": "First, the Plan", "paras": ["And as always (aal·wayz), the plan comes first. This is a real Claude (klaad) Code session, on the course test project.", "1. First, my request. Read CLAUDE.md and the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn), and give me the plan before writing any code. I don't tell it how to do it. I tell it what to read, and I ask for a plan. The specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) already says what each function has to do. And why ask for the plan first, if it'll write the code anyway? Because (buh·kuhz) fixing a plan takes a minute. Fixing code that's already written takes much longer. In the plan, you see the decisions (duh·si·zhnz) before they become files. And I told it which files to read, instead of explaining everything myself. The information (in·fr·may·shn) is already written in the project. My job is to tell it where to look.", "2. Second, its answer. One row per function, and how it will check each one. Look at this line: converting to BGR creates a new image, so the original photo stays the same. And this one: the slider only filters, so the model never runs again. The plan took less than a minute. And the real plan had more. For the page, it proposed a status bar above (uh·buhv) all the tabs, a list of runs with their (dhehr) mAP (em·ay·pee) and their (dhehr) reason, and the option to upload a photo or pick a test one. All of that was described before a single line was written.", "3. And here's what I check before I approve. Does it say how it will check each part? If a plan doesn't say how it will be checked, it isn't ready to approve. And if the plan doesn't say it, there's no need to argue. Just ask it to add how it'll check each function, and read it again. It's a short step, and it saves a lot of time later."]}, {"n": 15, "part": 1, "title": "What Claude Asked Me", "paras": ["And the end of the plan is the most interesting (in·truh·stuhng) part.", "1. Claude (klaad) asked me four (4) questions. The first was about the threshold (thresh·howld). It said: zero point two five (0.25) is also the default value, so the page can't tell your decision (duh·si·zhn) from a value nobody set. Please confirm it. The second: my run had no written reason, and I should write it myself, because (buh·kuhz) it's my reason. The other two (2) were about a short run, and about permissions (pr·mi·shnz). The first question (kwes·chn) is the most interesting (in·truh·stuhng) one. The threshold (thresh·howld) value matched the value the system uses when nobody chooses anything. So Claude (klaad) couldn't tell whether (weh·thr) I had chosen it, or whether (weh·thr) nobody had touched it. And instead of guessing, it asked. The third (thurd) question (kwes·chn) is a good one too. I had a test run of only two (2) epochs (eh·puhks), and Claude (klaad) asked whether (weh·thr) I wanted it in the list. It didn't delete it or hide it. It asked me, because (buh·kuhz) it's my folder.", "2. It followed CLAUDE.md. The threshold (thresh·howld) and the reason are yours. So I answered, wrote my reason myself, and approved the plan. That's what a well-written CLAUDE.md does. The rules from Module (maa·jool) one (1) to four (4) add up, and Claude (klaad) knows which decisions (duh·si·zhnz) are yours. And the fourth question (kwes·chn), about permissions (pr·mi·shnz), matters (ma·drz) too. Claude (klaad) doesn't run commands on your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) without your approval (uh·proo·vl). When it asks for permission, read the command before you accept."]}, {"n": 16, "part": 1, "title": "Then the Code, and the Proof", "paras": ["I approved it, and it wrote the code.", "1. First, the tests. Ten (10) out of ten (10), on the first try, in a little over three (3) minutes. Ten (10) out of ten (10) on the first try doesn't always (aal·wayz) happen. It happened here because (buh·kuhz) the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) was clear, and the plan said how each part would be checked. When the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) is vague, tests fail more, and that's useful information (in·fr·may·shn) too. And here's a real detail. The ten (10) detection (duh·tek·shn) tests passed, but the full set didn't. The three (3) README (reed·mee) tests failed, because (buh·kuhz) the README (reed·mee) wasn't written yet. Claude (klaad) said so clearly, explained (uhk·splaynd) that they weren't part of today's task, and didn't try to fix them quietly.", "2. Second, a real photo. It ran the model once, and then only filtered. Low (low) threshold (thresh·howld): two (2) potholes (paat·howlz). Medium: one (1). Very high: nothing (nuh·thuhng). And this matters (ma·drz). It didn't stop at the fake model. It tried the real model on a real photo, and showed me the result. The tests say the code does what the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) says. The real photo says the system works.", "3. And third (thurd), something I really liked. It pointed at a strange (straynj) box (baaks) in a corner of the photo, and told me: whether (weh·thr) that's a pothole (paat·howl) is for you to decide (duh·side). It didn't hide it. And it didn't decide (duh·side) for me. That box (baaks) was right in the corner of the photo, with a low (low) score, but above (uh·buhv) my threshold (thresh·howld). It could be a pothole (paat·howl) cut off by the edge, or it could be a false (fols) alarm. Looking at the photo, you decide (duh·side) in seconds."]}, {"n": 17, "part": 1, "title": "When a Test Fails", "paras": ["So far we've seen the tests pass. But what happens (ha·puhnz) when one fails? To see it, I changed (chaynjd) one single character in detect.py, on purpose (pur·puhs), in a copy of the project, and ran the tests.", "1. This is the real output. At the top, a row of dots, and the letter F in fifth place. Each dot is a test that passed. The F is the one that failed. And at the very bottom, the summary (suh·muh·ree): one (1) failed, nine (9) passed.", "2. First, which one failed? The output says it by name: test_keep, the test of the threshold (thresh·howld) filter. With that, I already know which function to look at. And that's the advantage (uhd·van·tuhj) of each test checking one single thing. The name of the test is almost (aal·mowst) the diagnosis.", "3. Second, what did it expect, and what did it get? It expected two (2) boxes (baak·suhz) with the threshold (thresh·howld) at zero point five (0.5): the one at zero point nine (0.9), and the one at exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) zero point five (0.5). It got only one. The line that starts with the letter E shows them side by side.", "4. And third (thurd), the cause. I had changed (chaynjd) greater than or equal to just greater than. With that change, a box (baaks) whose score is exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the threshold (thresh·howld) gets lost. It's one single character, and without the test nobody would have noticed, because (buh·kuhz) the page would still (stil) work. It would just show one box (baaks) less in some cases. That's why it's worth (wurth) reading the output calmly, in that order: which test, what it expected, what it got. Most of the time, the cause shows up by itself. And if you can't find the cause by reading, you can show Claude (klaad) the full output and ask it to explain what it means, without changing anything yet. Understand first, then fix."]}, {"n": 18, "part": 1, "title": "The Page, Running", "paras": ["And here's the page, running.", "1. First, at the top: the chosen run, its validation (va·luh·day·shn) mAP (em·ay·pee) fifty (50), and the reason it was trained (traynd) that way. You wrote that reason in Module (maa·jool) three (3). That line looks like a detail, but it answers the first question (kwes·chn) of anyone who uses the system: which model am I working with, and why that one?", "2. Second, the photo and the detections (duh·tek·shnz). On the left, the original. On the right, the boxes (baak·suhz) and their (dhehr) confidence (kaan·fuh·duhns). The first time you press Detect, it takes a few (fyoo) seconds, because (buh·kuhz) the model loads. After that, it's faster. And each box (baaks) shows the class and the score, for example pothole (paat·howl) and zero point eight seven (0.87). For someone new, that number says how sure the model is about each box (baaks), and it helps them understand why some boxes (baak·suhz) disappear when they move the slider.", "3. And third (thurd), the sentence, and a table with how many boxes (baak·suhz) there are of each class at that threshold (thresh·howld). Move the slider, and all three (3) change at once. And here's the reason for the whole first part of the class. The sentence, the table and the boxes (baak·suhz) come from the same saved predictions (pruh·dik·shnz). That's why the three (3) always (aal·wayz) agree. You'll (yool) never see a table that says two potholes (paat·howlz), and an image with three boxes (baak·suhz)."]}, {"n": 19, "part": 1, "title": "The Model Runs Once: gr.State", "paras": ["So how does the page avoid running the model again every time you move the slider? Here's the real code Claude (klaad) wrote.", "1. First, a special Gradio (gray·dee·ow) variable (veh·ree·uh·bl), called gr.State. It keeps all the predictions (pruh·dik·shnz) from the last time you pressed Detect. And look at the line in red. When the slider moves, the page only calls the function that filters and draws (draaz), with the saved predictions (pruh·dik·shnz). And Claude (klaad) added one more thing: the model is kept in memory after it's loaded the first time. That's why the second Detect is much faster than the first one. Loading the model is the slow part. Using it, not so much.", "2. In other words, gr.State remembers the predictions (pruh·dik·shnz) between clicks. The slider reads them, and filters. No model, no waiting. It's like taking a picture of all the boxes (baak·suhz) once, and after that only deciding which ones to show. The heavy work happens (ha·puhnz) once. The rest is choosing.", "3. And a very fine detail. When you change the photo, the old boxes (baak·suhz) are erased. So the boxes (baak·suhz) of one photo never appear on another one. The specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) didn't ask for that. Claude (klaad) thought (thaat) of it. But I checked it before accepting it. And think about why it matters (ma·drz). If the person changes the photo and still (stil) sees the old boxes (baak·suhz), they could believe (buh·leev) the system found (faund) potholes (paat·howlz) in a photo where there's nothing (nuh·thuhng)."]}, {"n": 20, "part": 1, "title": "Why Not a Global Variable?", "paras": ["And why not keep the predictions (pruh·dik·shnz) in a normal Python (pai·thaan) variable (veh·ree·uh·bl)? It looks simpler.", "1. This is the version with a global variable (veh·ree·uh·bl): one variable (veh·ree·uh·bl) for the whole app. As long as only one person uses the page, it works perfectly (pur·fuhkt·lee). That's why this mistake is so easy to make. On your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr), it never fails. And it's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) what you'd write first, because (buh·kuhz) that's how you learn Python (pai·thaan). A variable (veh·ree·uh·bl) at the top of the file, and every function uses it.", "2. And this is the version with gr.State. Gradio (gray·dee·ow) keeps a separate (seh·pruht) copy for each browser tab that opens the page. Each person has their (dhehr) own predictions (pruh·dik·shnz). And the code barely (behr·lee) changes. Instead of writing to a global variable (veh·ree·uh·bl), the function takes the state in and gives (givz) it back. Gradio (gray·dee·ow) handles the rest.", "3. Now picture two (2) people using the app at the same time, for example two people from the crew (kroo). With the global variable (veh·ree·uh·bl), the second one uploads a photo, and their (dhehr) result replaces the first one's. When the first person moves the slider, they see the boxes (baak·suhz) from someone else's photo. And nobody gets any error. With gr.State, each one sees only their (dhehr) own. It's the same lesson as BGR: the most dangerous (dayn·jr·uhs) mistakes (muh·stayks) are the ones that give no message at all. And this one only shows up when more than one person uses the system. In other words, exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) when it has become a system. And in the course this may seem far away, because (buh·kuhz) each of you uses your app alone. But in Class four point two (4.2) we'll talk about putting the system on a server. That's when this difference (di·fruhns) matters (ma·drz)."]}, {"n": 21, "part": 1, "title": "No Run Yet: the Page Must Not Crash", "paras": ["What happens (ha·puhnz) if someone opens your system before training anything?", "1. Look at the message. It doesn't crash. It says there's no run yet, explains when a run counts as finished, and says what to do. Look at how the message is written. It doesn't say error. It says what's missing (mi·suhng), how to know when it's ready, and what to do next. A message like that makes sense to someone who has never seen the code. And the status bar at the top changes too. The training part shows as not ready, with what to do. So the person sees the problem in two places, and both say the same thing.", "2. And Claude (klaad) really tested it. It built the app once with my runs, and once with an empty folder. Both worked. Think about it: the first person who opens your system has no runs. That's the very first screen they'll see. It has to work. And Claude (klaad) did this without me asking for it directly. The specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) said the page must not crash with no runs, and Claude (klaad) decided (duh·sai·duhd) to really test it, with an empty folder. That's the difference (di·fruhns) between reading a specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) and checking it."]}, {"n": 22, "part": 1, "title": "A Photo It Never Saw", "paras": ["And now, the real test.", "1. This photo isn't from the dataset (day·tuh·set). It's a street in another country. The model has never seen it. And it found (faund) the pothole (paat·howl). It's a very different photo: another camera (kam·ruh), other light, another kind of asphalt (as·fawlt). But the pothole (paat·howl) is big and dark, exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the kind our (aw·r) detector (duh·tek·tr) sees best. But don't get too excited about one single hit. One photo that works doesn't prove the system works. What it does prove is that the detector (duh·tek·tr) didn't only learn the style of the dataset's photos (fow·towz). To know more, you need many photos (fow·towz) like this one.", "2. So here's what I'm asking you to do. Test your system on at least one photo of your own, from your field, that isn't in your data. That's how you find out whether (weh·thr) your detector (duh·tek·tr) learned (lurnd) the object, or only learned (lurnd) your photos (fow·towz). And if it finds (faindz) nothing (nuh·thuhng), don't hide it. That result is gold for your \"What doesn't work\" section. And look for a photo that looks like how your system would really be used. If your system is for a car camera (kam·ruh), a photo from a car. If it's for a phone, a photo with your phone. That photo says more than any photo from the dataset (day·tuh·set)."]}, {"n": 23, "part": 1, "title": "Activity: Build Your Detection Page", "paras": ["All right, now it's your turn. This activity (ak·ti·vuh·dee) is the starting point of Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point one (4.1).", "1. First, copy the Module (maa·jool) four (4) update into your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), and add to project.yaml the threshold (thresh·howld) you chose in Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) three point two (3.2). Remember to copy the full update, hidden folders included. The .claude folder has the hook in it, and if it isn't copied, the hook will never run.", "2. Second, ask Claude (klaad) for the plan of src/detect.py and the page. And use a prompt like mine: read CLAUDE.md and the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn), and give me the plan before writing code. Change it the way you like, but keep that last part. The plan comes first.", "3. Third (thurd), read it, answer its questions, and approve it. The ten (10) tests have to pass. If Claude (klaad) asks about your threshold (thresh·howld) or your reason, answer it yourselves. Those are the two (2) decisions (duh·si·zhnz) the course asks of you, not of Claude (klaad).", "4. And fourth, run the app, and detect on one test photo and on one photo of your own. And if it finds (faindz) nothing (nuh·thuhng) on your photo, keep that screenshot (skreen·shaat). It isn't a failure. It's the first line of your section on what doesn't work."]}, {"n": 24, "part": 1, "title": "Wiring the Pages", "paras": ["Now we have three (3) pages. But three (3) separate (seh·pruht) pages aren't a system. So how do they connect?"]}, {"n": 25, "part": 1, "title": "The Output of One Page Is the Input of the Next", "paras": ["The idea is simple. What one page produces, the next one uses.", "1. First, the data page leaves the converted photos (fow·towz) in the data folder, under the name of your project. The folder name comes from project.yaml. That's why the project name has to be written once, in one single place. If one page uses one name and another page uses a different one, they can't find each other.", "2. Second, the training page reads that folder, and leaves best.pt in runs, together with the reason it was trained (traynd). The reason you wrote in Module (maa·jool) three (3) travels with the run. So when someone picks a run on page three (3), they know why it was trained (traynd) that way. And that reason is a simple text file, next to the run, with the same name. If a run doesn't have that file, the page shows an empty reason. That's why Claude (klaad) asked me to write it.", "3. Third (thurd), the detection (duh·tek·shn) page looks in runs for every finished run, each one with its reason. And this page doesn't ask anybody where the runs are. It looks for them by itself, in the same place where the previous (pree·vee·uhs) page left them. That's what it means for the pages to be connected.", "4. Nobody copies files by hand from one page to the next. Each page reads what the previous (pree·vee·uhs) one wrote. That's what makes it a system. In bigger (bi·gr) systems this is called a pipeline (paip·lain): data goes in at one end, passes (pa·suhz) through (throo) each stage, and comes out as a decision (duh·si·zhn). And for your project, this means you shouldn't move or rename the folders each page creates. If you move them by hand, the next page can't find them anymore."]}, {"n": 26, "part": 1, "title": "The App Tells You What Is Missing", "paras": ["And the app tells you what's missing (mi·suhng).", "1. Above (uh·buhv) the pages, the status of the system. When everything is ready, all three (3) are green. And when something is missing (mi·suhng), the light for that part changes color, and a sentence appears below it. The person doesn't have to guess which step they got stuck (stuhk) on.", "2. The function system_check looks for three (3) things: the converted data, a finished run, and the threshold (thresh·howld) figure (fi·gyr) from Module (maa·jool) three (3). And when one is missing (mi·suhng), it doesn't just say \"error\". It says what to do. For someone using your system for the first time, that's worth (wurth) a lot. It's like a car's dashboard. It doesn't explain the engine. It tells you which light is on, and what to do about it. A new user needs that, not a Python (pai·thaan) error message. And the check doesn't run the model or train anything. It only looks at whether (weh·thr) certain (sur·tn) files exist. That's why it's instant, and it can be shown above (uh·buhv) every page without making the app slow. And the third (thurd) part, the threshold (thresh·howld) figure (fi·gyr), comes from the Module (maa·jool) three (3) notebook. If you didn't run it, the bar says so, and says which notebook to run."]}, {"n": 27, "part": 1, "title": "When the Pages Are Not Wired", "paras": ["And what happens (ha·puhnz) when the pages aren't connected well? These are the three (3) most common (kaa·muhn) problems (praa·bluhmz).", "1. First, the list of runs is empty. Almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz), page three (3) is looking in a different folder from the one page two (2) writes to. That's why the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) says list_runs must read the same folder, runs. And how do you spot it? The list comes out empty, even though you know you trained (traynd). When that happens (ha·puhnz), don't look for the problem in the model. Look at which folder one page writes to, and which one the other page reads from.", "2. Second, the page crashes. For example, when there's no run yet, and nobody thought (thaat) about that case. The specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) covers it: no run, so the page says so and explains how to train one. The general rule (rool) is this. A page has to work even when the previous (pree·vee·uhs) page hasn't done its job yet. Instead of crashing, it explains what's missing (mi·suhng).", "3. And third (thurd), the threshold (thresh·howld) jumps back to a fixed value, because (buh·kuhz) the page ignores project.yaml. If any of these happens (ha·puhnz) to you, you know what to do: show Claude (klaad) the line of the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) that isn't met, and ask for one single change. And that advice matters (ma·drz): one line, and one change. If you ask Claude (klaad) to fix everything, it can change things that worked. If you show it the exact line of the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn), the change is small, and easy to check. And when you fix one of these, test the whole system again, not just that page. Sometimes fixing one connection breaks another."]}, {"n": 28, "part": 1, "title": "Reading a Traceback", "paras": ["And when the app doesn't even start, what you see is a traceback. It looks scary, but you always (aal·wayz) read it the same way.", "1. This one is real. I ran the app with the system's Python (pai·thaan), instead of the Python (pai·thaan) in the project's virtual (vur·choo·uhl) environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt). It's one of the most common (kaa·muhn) errors in the whole course. I shortened the long paths in the middle so they fit on the slide. On your screen they'll be longer, but the shape is the same.", "2. First, read it from the bottom. The last line says what happened. The lines above (uh·buhv) say where the program was when it happened. Many people start reading from the top, get lost in the paths, and never reach the line that matters (ma·drz). And the lines above (uh·buhv) aren't useless (yoos·luhs). They say which file and which line the program was on. When the error really is in the code, that line is where to start looking.", "3. Second, the last line says there's no module (maa·jool) called cv2, which is OpenCV. That doesn't mean the code is wrong (rong). It means this Python (pai·thaan) doesn't have the project's packages installed. Those packages live inside the virtual (vur·choo·uhl) environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt).", "4. And third (thurd), the fix. Activate the virtual (vur·choo·uhl) environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt), and run it again. The traceback doesn't say activate your environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt). You have to work that out. So when something like this happens (ha·puhnz), before you change any code, ask yourself: am I using the right Python (pai·thaan)? And if someone writes in the forum with this error, you already know what to answer. And on Windows, and on Mac too, there's an easy sign. When the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt) is active, the terminal (tur·muh·nl) shows .venv in parentheses at the start of the line. If you don't see it, the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt) isn't active."]}, {"n": 29, "part": 1, "title": "Check the Whole System Yourself", "paras": ["The tests pass. But there's something no test can do for you: use the whole system, the way another person would. This is step three (3) of the assignment (uh·sain·muhnt), and here's how to do it.", "1. First, close everything. The app, the terminal (tur·muh·nl), Claude (klaad) Code. Open a new terminal (tur·muh·nl), activate the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt), and start the app from the beginning. That way nothing (nuh·thuhng) saved from before can hide a problem from you. And start the app with the same command you wrote in the README (reed·mee). That way, you also check that the command works.", "2. Second, the data page. Your photos (fow·towz), your classes and your examples should appear. If something looks strange (straynj) here, everything after it will be wrong (rong) too.", "3. Third (thurd), the training page. Your finished run should appear, with the reason you wrote. You don't need to train again. Just confirm that the page finds (faindz) what already exists. And if you have more than one run, pick the one you'll (yool) use in your video. That way this walk-through is also practice for Class four point two (4.2).", "4. Fourth, the detection (duh·tek·shn) page. One test photo, and one photo of your own. The boxes (baak·suhz), the sentence and the table should appear, and they should agree. And on your own photo, don't worry if it finds (faindz) little. What matters (ma·drz) in this step isn't the result. It's that the page works from start to finish with a photo it has never seen.", "5. And fifth, move the slider. The boxes (baak·suhz) have to change instantly, with no waiting. If every move takes time, the model is running again, and that's a mistake, even if the tests pass. Take screenshots (skreen·shaats) of each step as you go. Those are exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the screenshots (skreen·shaats) I ask for in the assignment's PDF."]}, {"n": 30, "part": 1, "title": "Break 1 · 5 Minutes", "paras": ["Let's take a five (5) minute break. Stand up, stretch (strech), and rest your eyes. When we come back: the last Claude (klaad) tool, hooks."]}, {"n": 31, "part": 1, "title": "Hooks, the Last Claude Tool", "paras": ["All right, let's continue. I promised you we'd meet the last Claude (klaad) Code tool this week. Here it is: hooks."]}, {"n": 32, "part": 1, "title": "Your Four Claude Tools", "paras": ["First, the tools you already know.", "1. Module (maa·jool) one (1): CLAUDE.md, the rules Claude (klaad) reads when it starts. That file is the base of everything. It holds the rules Claude (klaad) reads every time it starts, like not changing the threshold (thresh·howld), and not uploading data to GitHub (git·huhb).", "2. Module (maa·jool) two (2): Skills, a task Claude (klaad) knows how to do, like reviewing (ree·vyoo·uhng) your dataset (day·tuh·set). A Skill is like a saved recipe. Instead of explaining the same task every time, Claude (klaad) finds (faindz) it written down, and follows it the same way every time.", "3. Module (maa·jool) three (3): subagents (suhb·ay·jnts), a reviewer (ree·vyoo·wr) with fresh eyes that never saw your conversation (kaan·vr·say·shn). What makes the subagent (suhb·ay·jnt) useful is that it doesn't know the conversation (kaan·vr·say·shn). It looks at the work the way another person would, without knowing what you meant.", "4. And today, Module (maa·jool) four (4): hooks. Code that always (aal·wayz) runs, at a fixed moment, without anybody having to remember it. And the four (4) work together. CLAUDE.md sets the rules. The Skill does a task. The subagent (suhb·ay·jnt) reviews. And the hook makes sure nothing (nuh·thuhng) important (uhm·por·tnt) gets skipped. And in your project you already have all of them. CLAUDE.md since Module (maa·jool) one (1), the dataset (day·tuh·set) Skill since Module (maa·jool) two (2), the image-auditor subagent (suhb·ay·jnt) since Module (maa·jool) three (3), and today the hook. They're all in the project folder, and they're all files you can open and read."]}, {"n": 33, "part": 1, "title": "What a Hook Is", "paras": ["So what exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) is a hook?", "1. First, Claude (klaad) decides to run a command. For example, git commit. In our (aw·r) case, the hook only cares about commits. But the same idea works for any command: before something happens (ha·puhnz), a script checks that it's safe.", "2. Second, before that command runs, the hook runs. Ours is a small Python (pai·thaan) script: pre_commit_check.py. That script lives in your project, in the .claude folder. It isn't part of Claude (klaad). It's your code, and you can read it and change it like any other file.", "3. And third (thurd), the hook answers. Exit code zero (0): the command goes ahead. Exit code two (2): the command is blocked, and Claude (klaad) reads a message that explains why. The hook doesn't decide (duh·side) for you, or for Claude (klaad). It checks one very clear condition, and says yes or no, with a reason. And the number two (2) isn't random. Claude (klaad) Code reads that code as block. Any other error from the script doesn't block. It's just shown. That's why the script is written to exit with two only when the commit really has to stop."]}, {"n": 34, "part": 1, "title": "A Rule Asks. A Hook Enforces.", "paras": ["And how is that different from a rule (rool) in CLAUDE.md?", "1. First, a rule (rool). CLAUDE.md says that runs and .pt files never get uploaded. Claude (klaad) reads it. And it almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) follows it. But it's an instruction written in words, and Claude (klaad) interprets it. If the conversation (kaan·vr·say·shn) is long, or the request is confusing, it can happen that it doesn't apply it.", "2. Second, a hook. It lives in .claude/settings.json, and it tells Claude (klaad) Code: before every terminal (tur·muh·nl) command, run our (aw·r) script. The hook isn't written in words. It's a command. Claude (klaad) Code runs it before every terminal (tur·muh·nl) command, and there's no way for Claude (klaad) to decide (duh·side) to skip it. And that file also says when to run the script: before Claude (klaad) uses the terminal (tur·muh·nl). That's why the hook sees every command, even though it only acts on commits.", "3. And here's the difference (di·fruhns). Claude (klaad) almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) follows a rule (rool). A hook always (aal·wayz) runs, whatever (wuh·teh·vr) Claude (klaad) decides. The rule (rool) asks. The hook enforces. Why isn't the rule (rool) enough (uh·nuhf)? Because (buh·kuhz) Claude (klaad) is a language model. \"Almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz)\" isn't \"always (aal·wayz)\". And for things that must never happen, like uploading a private data file, you use a hook. And one doesn't replace the other. We use both. The rule (rool) explains to Claude (klaad) why it shouldn't do it, and that's almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) enough (uh·nuhf). The hook is there for the rare case when it isn't."]}, {"n": 35, "part": 1, "title": "Our Hook: pre_commit_check.py", "paras": ["This is the hook that comes in your kit.", "1. It blocks a commit when it includes (uhn·kloodz) the folders data, runs, weights (wayts) or the virtual (vur·choo·uhl) environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt), any model weights (wayts) file, or a file over ten (10) megabytes (meh·guh·baits). It also blocks it when the tests fail, except the README (reed·mee) tests, which we'll see in a moment. Why ten (10) megabytes (meh·guh·baits)? Because (buh·kuhz) GitHub (git·huhb) isn't made for big files. And once a big file is in the history (his·tr·ee), it stays there forever, even if you delete it later. And why check the tests too? Because (buh·kuhz) a commit is a promise (praa·muhs). It says the code at that point works. If the tests fail, that promise (praa·muhs) is false (fols), and the next person who clones the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee) inherits the problem. And it checks the files that are staged for the commit, not every file in the folder. That's why you can keep your data and weights (wayts) in the folder with no problem. It only blocks if you try to upload them.", "2. So what does it protect? The rule (rool) from Module (maa·jool) three (3): data and weights (wayts) never go to GitHub (git·huhb). And it keeps broken code out. And if you ever need to change the size limit, it's written in the script, on one line, and you can read it. But think carefully (kehr·fuh·lee) first, because (buh·kuhz) the limit is there for a reason."]}, {"n": 36, "part": 1, "title": "What the Hook Receives, and What It Answers", "paras": ["Let's look a little closer at how the hook talks to Claude (klaad) Code.", "1. First, what it receives. Claude (klaad) Code sends the script a short text in JSON format: the name of the tool, and the command Claude (klaad) wants to run. Here, in red: git commit. JSON is the same text format many applications use to send each other information (in·fr·may·shn). It's pairs of names and values, between curly brackets. You don't need to learn it by heart. Just know that the script reads it, finds (faindz) the command, and decides.", "2. Second, what it answers. Exit code zero (0): the command runs. Exit code two (2): it's blocked, and Claude (klaad) reads the message. And the message that comes with the block isn't for you. It's for Claude (klaad). That's why it has to say exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) which file is the problem, and what to do, because (buh·kuhz) Claude (klaad) will read it and act on that information (in·fr·may·shn).", "3. And one detail, so it never gets in your way. Any command that isn't git commit passes (pa·suhz) in milliseconds (mi·luh·seh·kuhndz). Only commits are checked."]}, {"n": 37, "part": 1, "title": "Turning It On", "paras": ["And how do you turn it on?", "1. The kit copies two (2) files: settings.json, which says when to run the hook, and the script, which says what to check. One warning (wor·nuhng): if your project already had a settings.json, the kit replaces it. If you had something in it, move it into the new one. And on a Mac, the .claude folder doesn't show in Finder, because (buh·kuhz) it starts with a dot. To see it, press Command, Shift and period. On Windows, in File Explorer, turn on the option to show hidden items.", "2. And a step people often forget. After copying them, close Claude (klaad) Code and open it again in your project folder, so it reads the hook. And since the hook uses the python (pai·thaan) command, activate your virtual (vur·choo·uhl) environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt) before you open Claude (klaad) Code, as always (aal·wayz). If the hook does nothing (nuh·thuhng) after you open it again, the first thing to check is which folder you opened Claude (klaad) Code in. It has to be the project folder, the one with the .claude folder inside. If after all this the hook still (stil) doesn't run, check the settings.json file. A small mistake, like an extra comma, means Claude (klaad) Code can't read it."]}, {"n": 38, "part": 1, "title": "Test 1: Commit My Model", "paras": ["Now, does it work? This is a real session. I added my best.pt file to the commit on purpose (pur·puhs), and asked Claude (klaad) to commit it.", "1. First, my request: make the commit, with the message you see. I did it on purpose (pur·puhs), because (buh·kuhz) I wanted to see what happens (ha·puhnz) in the worst case. The file was already staged, so the only thing missing (mi·suhng) was the command.", "2. Second, its answer. It refused. The only thing staged was a weights (wayts) file, the project rules forbid it, and the hook exists to block exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) that. Then it gave me the command to take the file out of the commit. And it didn't just say no. It explained (uhk·splaynd) what was staged for the commit, why it shouldn't go up, and gave me the exact command to take it out. That's what you expect from a good teammate.", "3. And here's something curious. The rule (rool) was enough (uh·nuhf). Claude (klaad) never even tried, so the hook never had to run. And that's what we want. The hook is there for the day something fails, not to work every day. If the hook blocks very often, something in the way of working needs checking."]}, {"n": 39, "part": 1, "title": "Test 2: Try It Once", "paras": ["So I asked for something different: try it once, so we can see the hook at work.", "1. First, my request: run the commit just once, and tell me what the hook answered. Look at how the request is written. I tell it exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) what I want: just once, and tell me what happened. I don't ask it to fix it, or to try another way.", "2. Second, the answer. The hook blocked the commit. Here, in red, is its message: which file, why it can't go to GitHub (git·huhb), and how to fix it. And Claude (klaad) explains that git never even ran. And look at what Claude (klaad) did NOT do. It didn't try to get around the hook. It didn't look for another way to upload the file. That's in CLAUDE.md too: if the hook blocks, you fix the cause.", "3. So there are two (2) layers. The rule (rool) in CLAUDE.md, and the hook behind it. If the rule (rool) fails, the hook doesn't. In the assignment (uh·sain·muhnt), I'm asking you for a screenshot (skreen·shaat) of this block. And for the screenshot (skreen·shaat), you don't need to repeat my test with your model. You can stage any big file, or any .pt file, and ask Claude (klaad) to commit it once. Then you take it out with the command the message itself gives (givz) you."]}, {"n": 40, "part": 1, "title": "The Hook Also Runs the Tests", "paras": ["And the hook doesn't only look for forbidden files. It also runs the tests.", "1. First, I asked Claude (klaad) for a normal commit: just some changes to LOG.md. I chose a small change on purpose (pur·puhs). I wanted to see the normal case, the one that will happen every day, not just the case where it blocks.", "2. Second, its answer. It made the commit, and the hook didn't block it. And look at how normal it looks. The hook works quietly when everything is fine. You only see it when something is wrong (rong). That's how good checks should be.", "3. So what happened inside? Before that commit, the hook ran every test except the README (reed·mee) ones. They passed, so the commit went ahead. If one had failed, the commit would have stopped, and Claude (klaad) would have received the lines of the error. That's how broken code stays away from GitHub (git·huhb). And there's a real detail from our (aw·r) test. While the README (reed·mee) wasn't written, its tests failed. That's why the hook ignores the README (reed·mee) tests. If not, nobody could commit until (uhn·tel) the README (reed·mee) was done, and that doesn't make sense."]}, {"n": 41, "part": 1, "title": "What Hooks Are Good For", "paras": ["So what are hooks good for, and what aren't they good for?", "1. They're good for checks that must happen every single time. Checks that are fast: seconds, not minutes. And that give a clear message: what's wrong (rong), and how to fix it. In industry (in·duh·stree), they're used to check the format of the code before every commit, or to stop a password from slipping into GitHub (git·huhb) by mistake. And the password case is very real. There are tools that check every commit for things that look like keys, and block the commit if they find one. It's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the same idea as our (aw·r) hook.", "2. They're not good for storing passwords inside the script, for long trainings or downloads, or for deciding things that are yours to decide (duh·side). Why not for long trainings? Because (buh·kuhz) a hook that takes an hour blocks all the work. People turn it off, and a check that's turned off protects nothing (nuh·thuhng). A good hook is fast, so nobody feels like removing it."]}, {"n": 42, "part": 1, "title": "What Goes to GitHub, and What Doesn't", "paras": ["The hook blocks what shouldn't go up. But there's a layer before the hook, and it's the first one that protects your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee): the .gitignore file.", "1. This is part of the kit's .gitignore. It tells Git which folders and files to ignore: the data, the runs, the weights (wayts), and any model file. Whatever (wuh·teh·vr) is listed here doesn't even show up when Git looks for changes. That's why, to put my model into the hook test, I had to force it in on purpose (pur·puhs). And look at the data line. It ignores everything in the data folder, except a small file that explains where to put the photos (fow·towz). That way the folder exists in the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), but empty.", "2. And why so much care about size? Because (buh·kuhz) GitHub (git·huhb) has a limit. It refuses any file over a hundred (100) megabytes (meh·guh·baits), and it warns you from fifty (50). A big model or a folder of photos (fow·towz) goes over that very fast. And as we said, once something big gets into the history (his·tr·ee), it stays there, even if you delete it later.", "3. So where do your data and weights (wayts) live? On your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr). And the README (reed·mee) says how to get them back: where to download (daun·lowd) the dataset (day·tuh·set), and how to train the model. That way, another person can rebuild everything without you uploading a single heavy file. The repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee) keeps the code and the instructions (uhn·struhk·shnz). The data gets rebuilt. And before your last push, run git status. If you see a data folder or a .pt file in the list, something is wrong (rong) in the .gitignore. It takes a minute, and it saves you a big problem."]}, {"n": 43, "part": 1, "title": "Small Commits, Clear Messages", "paras": ["And since we're talking about commits, one more thing: the messages. In the assignment (uh·sain·muhnt), clean commits are part of the grade. So what does clean mean?", "1. On the left, a history (his·tr·ee) you see a lot. Update. Fix. Changes. Final. Final two. A week later, not even the person who wrote them knows what changed (chaynjd) in each one. And if something breaks, there's no way to know when it happened. And also, when your professor or a classmate (klas·mayt) looks at the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), the history (his·tr·ee) is the first thing that tells how you worked. A history (his·tr·ee) like that tells nothing (nuh·thuhng).", "2. On the right, the same week of work, told a different way. Each message says what changed (chaynjd), and where. Add detect.py. Wire page three to the runs. Install steps in the README (reed·mee) for Windows and Mac. If something breaks, you read the history (his·tr·ee) and find the suspicious commit in seconds. And a good rule (rool) for the message: it should complete the sentence this commit will. Add detect.py. Wire page three. If the message completes that sentence, it's almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) clear.", "3. And also, small commits. One commit per idea, not one giant commit at the end of the week. That way, each one goes through (throo) the hook, with its tests, and if one fails, the problem is in a few (fyoo) files. And if you ask Claude (klaad) to make the commit, ask it for a message that says what changed (chaynjd). It does that very well. But read the message before you approve it, the same way you read the code. And if a commit goes wrong (rong), it's fine. You can fix it with another commit. What you can't easily do is remove a big or private file from the history (his·tr·ee). That's why the hook checks first."]}, {"n": 44, "part": 1, "title": "The README", "paras": ["We have a system that works on your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr). Now, the hard question (kwes·chn): does it work on somebody else's? That's what the README (reed·mee) is for."]}, {"n": 45, "part": 1, "title": "From Nothing: the Classmate Test", "paras": ["Here's a simple test to know if your README (reed·mee) works. We'll call it the test from nothing (nuh·thuhng).", "1. First, clone your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee) into a new, empty folder. It has to be a really new folder. If you clone inside the folder you already have, everything that's already there, like the virtual (vur·choo·uhl) environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt) or the data, will hide the problems (praa·bluhmz) from you.", "2. Second, follow your README (reed·mee) word by word. Only what it says, nothing (nuh·thuhng) you remember. And this is harder than it sounds. You know things you never wrote down. For example, that you have to activate the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt) before every command. If it isn't written, for the other person it doesn't exist.", "3. Third (thurd), every time you have to do something the README (reed·mee) doesn't say, add it. And write down every time you had to guess something. Each one of those notes is a line the README (reed·mee) is missing (mi·suhng).", "4. And then, the real test. A classmate (klas·mayt) tries it, through (throo) the forum. Wherever (weh·reh·vr) your classmate (klas·mayt) gets stuck (stuhk), your README (reed·mee) is missing (mi·suhng) something. And when your classmate (klas·mayt) replies, don't argue. If they got stuck (stuhk), it's because (buh·kuhz) the README (reed·mee) was missing (mi·suhng) something, even if it seems obvious (aab·vee·uhs) to you. Add it, and thank them."]}, {"n": 46, "part": 1, "title": "Seven Sections: docs/readme_spec.md", "paras": ["The README (reed·mee) specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn) asks for seven (7) sections, in this order.", "1. What it does, how to install it, how to get the data, how to train, how to detect, the results, and last, what doesn't work. Why this order? Because (buh·kuhz) it's the order in which someone new needs them. First, what is this? Then, how do I install it? Then the data, and so on. And the last section is the most honest (aa·nuhst) one. And think about the results section. It isn't there to show off. It's there so the other person knows what to expect. If you say how the detector (duh·tek·tr) did on your test set, they know whether (weh·thr) their (dhehr) result is normal, or whether (weh·thr) something went wrong (rong). And the section names have to be exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) those, in English, because (buh·kuhz) the tests look for them that way. If you change a heading, the test won't find it.", "2. Three (3) tests check the sections, the commands, and that the last section isn't empty. And the hook doesn't run those three. So you can commit your code before your README (reed·mee) is ready. And look at the third (thurd) test, the one that checks the last section isn't empty. It's the only test in the course that checks something only you can write. And you can run just those three (3) tests while you write, with the test_readme file. That way you know when the README (reed·mee) meets the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn), without waiting for the other tests."]}, {"n": 47, "part": 1, "title": "Claude Drafts It", "paras": ["I asked Claude (klaad) for a first draft of the README (reed·mee). Another real session.", "1. First, my request. Follow the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn). Use only facts you can check. Where you can't, write TODO. And leave the \"What doesn't work\" section for me. That last instruction is important (uhm·por·tnt). If I don't give it, Claude (klaad) can write a list of limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz) that sound right, but nobody checked. That section has to come from what I saw.", "2. Second, its answer. It left three (3) things for me to fill in. And it found (faund) the training time in the results. I had told it to leave that as a TODO, but it found (faund) the real number, and asked me to confirm that run was on my computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr). It didn't make the number up, and it didn't leave it unchecked. But look at the line in red. On a new clone, the setup check never says READY, because (buh·kuhz) the Module (maa·jool) one (1) photo folder is empty. And it also found (faund) where each number in the README (reed·mee) came (kaym) from. The test results, from the Module (maa·jool) three (3) notebook. Precision (pruh·si·zhn) and recall at my threshold (thresh·howld), from the thresholds file. And the size of each part of the dataset (day·tuh·set), from the Module (maa·jool) two (2) notebook. Every number in the README (reed·mee) has a source you can check.", "3. That's a real problem, and I had never noticed it. And it goes in the README (reed·mee). Why did I never see it? Because (buh·kuhz) in my folder those photos (fow·towz) had been there since Module (maa·jool) one (1). Only a new, empty folder shows what will happen to another person. And the draft didn't write the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee) address, because (buh·kuhz) it couldn't find it. It left a TODO. That's better than making up an address that doesn't exist."]}, {"n": 48, "part": 1, "title": "Claude Drafts. You Check Every Command.", "paras": ["Claude (klaad) writes the draft. You check every command.", "1. First, a README (reed·mee) nobody can follow. \"Install everything and run the app.\" Install what? With which version of Python (pai·thaan)? And the worst part is that, to whoever wrote it, it seems like enough (uh·nuhf), because (buh·kuhz) they already know what to install. A bad README (reed·mee) almost (aal·mowst) never looks bad from the inside.", "2. Second, a README (reed·mee) a classmate (klas·mayt) can follow. Every command, in order, from cloning to the setup check. Even the Python (pai·thaan) version is there. That isn't a detail. Some libraries don't work with every version, and if the other person has a different one, the installation can fail without them knowing why.", "3. Every command, for Windows and for Mac. And one rule (rool) for you: if you didn't run it, don't write it. On Windows, activating the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt) uses a different path. On Mac and Linux, you use source. If your README (reed·mee) only has one version, half your classmates will get stuck (stuhk) at the second command."]}, {"n": 49, "part": 1, "title": "The Test From Nothing, Command by Command", "paras": ["Let's go back to the test from nothing (nuh·thuhng), but now command by command, on Windows and on Mac, because (buh·kuhz) this is where most people get stuck (stuhk).", "1. First, clone. It's the same on both systems. Then create the virtual (vur·choo·uhl) environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt). Here they start to differ. On Windows you use the py launcher, with the Python (pai·thaan) version. On a Mac you call python (pai·thaan) three point twelve (3.12) directly. Then activate the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt), which also differs. On Windows, the path has backslashes, and the folder is called Scripts. On a Mac you use source, and the folder is called bin. And the last two, installing the requirements (ruh·kwai·r·muhnts) and running the project check, are the same again. That's why your README (reed·mee) needs both versions. A classmate (klas·mayt) on Windows can't guess the Mac path. And one more thing. On Windows, the command for Python (pai·thaan) is sometimes py and sometimes python (pai·thaan), depending on how it was installed. If your classmate (klas·mayt) uses a different one, the README (reed·mee) can give both options.", "2. And the most important (uhm·por·tnt) part: do it in a new folder, in a new terminal (tur·muh·nl). Your old terminal (tur·muh·nl) remembers things. It may already have the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt) active, or be sitting in another folder. A new terminal (tur·muh·nl) is like your classmate's (klas·mayts): empty. And one thing on Windows that happens (ha·puhnz) a lot. Sometimes PowerShell won't let you activate the environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt), and shows an error about scripts. If that happens (ha·puhnz), don't hide it. Write it in the README (reed·mee), with what you did to fix it. That's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the kind of thing that stops a classmate (klas·mayt). And if something fails in this test, don't fix it silently. Fix it, and add the missing (mi·suhng) line to the README (reed·mee). The goal isn't that it works in your new folder. It's that it works in your classmate's (klas·mayts)."]}, {"n": 50, "part": 1, "title": "What a Fresh Look Found in Our README", "paras": ["And here's what a fresh look found (faund) in our (aw·r) own README (reed·mee), from Claude's draft and from testing it from nothing (nuh·thuhng).", "1. First, the setup check never says READY on a new clone, because (buh·kuhz) the Module (maa·jool) one (1) photo folder is empty. Second, the requirements (ruh·kwai·r·muhnts) file is pinned for an Intel Mac, and nobody tested it on Windows, Linux or a newer Mac. Third (thurd), the Windows commands are the standard ones, but nobody ran them. And fourth, the address to clone the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee) was missing (mi·suhng). Look at the third (thurd) one. The Windows commands being the standard ones doesn't mean they work. If nobody ran them, we don't know. And an honest (aa·nuhst) README (reed·mee) says exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) that, instead of promising something nobody tested. And Claude (klaad) found (faund) two (2) more things in the dataset (day·tuh·set): one box (baaks) that went outside the image, and several (sev·ruhl) very small boxes (baak·suhz). Those aren't code errors either, but they're things someone using the system should know.", "2. None of these is a bug in the detector (duh·tek·tr). But every one of them stops a classmate (klas·mayt). That's why the test from nothing (nuh·thuhng) matters (ma·drz) so much. And the good news is that each one is fixed with one or two lines in the README (reed·mee). You don't need to change the code. You need to tell the other person what they need to know."]}, {"n": 51, "part": 1, "title": "Declaring What Doesn't Work", "paras": ["And now, the part of this class that matters (ma·drz) most to me. Saying what doesn't work."]}, {"n": 52, "part": 1, "title": "Say What Doesn't Work", "paras": ["There's one idea in the syllabus (si·luh·buhs) I want you to take with you.", "1. A user who knows the system misses small potholes (paat·howlz) checks them with their (dhehr) own eyes. A user who doesn't know trusts an empty photo. Now think about your field. A doctor who knows a system fails on a certain (sur·tn) kind of image looks at those images (i·muh·juhz) more carefully (kehr·fuh·lee). A doctor who doesn't know, doesn't. The difference (di·fruhns) between the two users isn't the system. The system is the same. The difference (di·fruhns) is what they know about it. And that depends on what you write.", "2. So saying what doesn't work doesn't make your work incomplete. Hiding it does. And this isn't just for the assignment (uh·sain·muhnt). Serious systems publish their (dhehr) limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz). That's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) why people trust them. Think about the leaflet of any medicine. It says what it's for, but also when not to take it. Nobody thinks the medicine is bad because (buh·kuhz) of that. The opposite: people trust it more."]}, {"n": 53, "part": 1, "title": "Our System: What Doesn't Work", "paras": ["Here are the limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz) of our (aw·r) own reference system.", "1. First, this photo from Module (maa·jool) three (3). Green: potholes (paat·howlz) it found (faund). Red: a false (fols) alarm. Yellow: potholes (paat·howlz) it missed (mist). And the yellow ones have something in common (kaa·muhn): they're shallow (sha·low) and dry. The red one is a second box (baaks) on a pothole (paat·howl) it had already found (faund). And these observations don't come from a number. They come from looking at the images (i·muh·juhz), as we did in Module (maa·jool) three (3). The mAP (em·ay·pee) tells us the detector (duh·tek·tr) isn't perfect. Only the images (i·muh·juhz) tell us which potholes (paat·howlz) it misses. And that photo also teaches something about the numbers (nuhm·brz). The red false (fols) alarm is actually (ak·choo·uh·lee) on a pothole (paat·howl). The problem isn't that it saw something that doesn't exist. It's that it counted it twice.", "2. And here's what our (aw·r) README (reed·mee) says. It misses shallow (sha·low), dry or far-away potholes (paat·howlz). It sometimes puts a second box (baaks) on the same pothole (paat·howl), which counts as a false (fols) alarm. And we only tested it on daytime photos (fow·towz), never at night or in the rain. Look at how it's written. It doesn't say \"the detector (duh·tek·tr) is bad\". It says exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) when it fails, and why. That's what helps the person who uses it. And look at the daytime-only line. It doesn't say it doesn't work at night. It says it was never tested. That's an important (uhm·por·tnt) difference (di·fruhns). Knowing it fails is one thing. Not knowing is another."]}, {"n": 54, "part": 1, "title": "A Limitation, Written Well", "paras": ["You know where to find your limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz). Now, how do you write them well? Because (buh·kuhz) the same limitation (li·muh·tay·shn) can be written in a way that helps nobody, or in a way that does.", "1. This is the way that doesn't help. It may not work in some cases. That's true, but it's true for every system in the world. The person reading it doesn't know which cases, or what to do. And it's the most common (kaa·muhn) sentence in students' READMEs. It's written to tick a box (baaks), but it helps nobody.", "2. And this is the way that helps. It has three (3) parts. First, what fails: it often misses shallow (sha·low), dry potholes (paat·howlz). Second, the evidence (eh·vuh·duhns): the yellow boxes (baak·suhz) in my test photos (fow·towz), with an image. And third (thurd), what the user should do: check shallow (sha·low) potholes (paat·howlz) by eye. And the evidence (eh·vuh·duhns) also makes the whole README (reed·mee) more believable. If the person sees that your limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz) have proof, they believe (buh·leev) your results more.", "3. What fails, the evidence (eh·vuh·duhns), and what the user should do. With those three (3) parts, any limitation (li·muh·tay·shn) becomes useful. And the evidence (eh·vuh·duhns) doesn't have to be a number. It can be an image, or a test you ran. What matters (ma·drz) is that the person can see you didn't make it up. And if you don't have the evidence (eh·vuh·duhns) yet, say so: I didn't test it at night. That's an honest (aa·nuhst) limitation (li·muh·tay·shn) too. And a limitation (li·muh·tay·shn) written like this also helps whoever wants to improve the system later. They know exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) where to start: more photos (fow·towz) of shallow (sha·low), dry potholes (paat·howlz)."]}, {"n": 55, "part": 1, "title": "Where to Find Yours", "paras": ["So where do you find the limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz) of your own system? In three (3) places.", "1. First, in your wrong (rong) examples from Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) three point two (3.2), and in what image-auditor found (faund). If you saw that your detector (duh·tek·tr) confuses two (2) classes, or fails on small objects (aab·jekts), that's already a limitation (li·muh·tay·shn). You just have to write it for the user. And if you used image-auditor in Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) three point two (3.2), that report (ruh·port) is a gold mine. Read what it found (faund), keep what you can also see in the images (i·muh·juhz), and turn it into sentences (sen·tn·suhz) for the user.", "2. Second, in the test from nothing (nuh·thuhng): whatever (wuh·teh·vr) broke in the new folder. And even if it has nothing (nuh·thuhng) to do with the detector (duh·tek·tr), it goes in the README (reed·mee). If the installation fails, the person never even gets to see the detector (duh·tek·tr).", "3. And third (thurd), in your classmate (klas·mayt). Wherever (weh·reh·vr) they got stuck (stuhk), your system doesn't explain something, or doesn't do it well. And don't stop at what they said. Ask yourself why they got stuck (stuhk). Often the cause is an assumption of yours that you never wrote down."]}, {"n": 56, "part": 1, "title": "Activity: Run Your System for Someone Else", "paras": ["Now the activity (ak·ti·vuh·dee) with a classmate (klas·mayt). It's part of Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point one (4.1), and it happens (ha·puhnz) in the new forum, Run My System.", "1. First, write your README (reed·mee), with the seven (7) sections.", "2. Second, clone it into a new folder, and follow it word by word. Do it yourselves first, before you share (shehr) it. That way your classmate (klas·mayt) finds (faindz) the problems (praa·bluhmz) you can't see, not the ones you could have fixed on your own.", "3. Third (thurd), by Wednesday (wenz·day) at the latest, share (shehr) it in the forum. Sharing in the forum means opening (ow·puh·nuhng) a topic with the link to your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), and one sentence about what your system does. That way your classmate (klas·mayt) knows what to expect before starting.", "4. And fourth, by Thursday (thurz·day) at the latest, follow a classmate's (klas·mayts) README (reed·mee) and reply with every point where you got stuck (stuhk). Pick a topic with few (fyoo) replies, so nobody is left without help. And be specific (spuh·si·fik). Not \"everything is fine\". The exact step, and the exact error. That's what helps. And when you reply, also write which system you were on: Windows or Mac. A step that works on one can fail on the other, and your classmate (klas·mayt) needs to know that to fix it."]}, {"n": 57, "part": 1, "title": "Your LOG.md for Module 4", "paras": ["And as in every module (maa·jool), your LOG.md.", "1. Here's an example for Module (maa·jool) four (4). The plan of detect.py, approved after answering its questions, with the ten (10) tests. The hook blocking the model, and the normal commit that went through (throo). What Claude (klaad) found (faund) in the README (reed·mee). What you had to add in the test from nothing (nuh·thuhng), and where your classmate (klas·mayt) got stuck (stuhk). And the last line, in red: \"What doesn't work\" is written by you. Each line says something concrete. It doesn't say I worked on the README (reed·mee). It says what was found (faund), what changed (chaynjd), and why. That way, when someone reads the LOG, they understand how you worked. And write the LOG on the same day. By the end of the week, nobody remembers what Claude (klaad) asked on Monday (muhn·day), or why they answered the way they did.", "2. What you ask, what you check, what you change. A few (fyoo) lines per session, as always (aal·wayz). And the LOG helps you too. When you write the assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) PDF, half the work is already there, in order by date."]}, {"n": 58, "part": 1, "title": "Assignment 4.1: The Fully Functional System", "paras": ["And that brings (bringz) us to Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point one (4.1): the fully functional system.", "1. Six (6) steps. Add the update and your threshold (thresh·howld). Build src/detect.py and the page, with its ten (10) tests. Check the whole system yourself. See the hook block a commit with a model. Write your README (reed·mee), with all seventy-five (75) tests passing. And the test from nothing (nuh·thuhng): first you, then a classmate (klas·mayt). And the third (thurd) step, checking the whole system yourself, is the one people forget most. It isn't enough (uh·nuhf) for the tests to pass. Open the app from the start, go through (throo) the three (3) pages, and confirm with your own eyes that everything works together. I showed you how, step by step, a few (fyoo) slides ago.", "2. And one reminder. \"What doesn't work\" is yours. Claude (klaad) can find problems (praa·bluhmz), but you decide (duh·side) what goes in that section. And write it in your own words. If you copy the list Claude (klaad) gave you without checking it, it shows, because (buh·kuhz) it talks about things you didn't see."]}, {"n": 59, "part": 1, "title": "Assignment 4.1: What to Submit", "paras": ["And what do you submit?", "1. Two (2) things. As online text, the link to your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee). And one PDF with screenshots (skreen·shaats) of the three (3) pages working, the hook blocking a commit, what you added to the README (reed·mee) in the test from nothing (nuh·thuhng), and what your classmate (klas·mayt) told you. And please: no data, no runs, no weights (wayts) files in the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee). The hook helps, but check with git status before your last push anyway. And in the PDF, each screenshot (skreen·shaat) with one sentence under it, saying what it shows. A screenshot (skreen·shaat) alone explains nothing (nuh·thuhng). The sentence says what I'm looking at, and why it matters (ma·drz).", "2. And here's the rubric (roo·brik). What counts most: the detection (duh·tek·shn) page with its tests, the whole system working, and the README (reed·mee). And \"What doesn't work\" has its own weight (wayt), because (buh·kuhz) it matters (ma·drz). And the classmate (klas·mayt) part counts even if your classmate (klas·mayt) found (faund) nothing (nuh·thuhng). What matters (ma·drz) is that they tried it, and that you report (ruh·port) what happened."]}, {"n": 60, "part": 1, "title": "Common Problems", "paras": ["Before we finish, the problems (praa·bluhmz) you're most likely to hit, and what to do.", "1. The list of runs is empty? Train on page two (2), or check that best.pt exists. The hook never runs? Close Claude (klaad) Code and open it again in the project folder. The README (reed·mee) tests fail? That's normal until (uhn·tel) you write it. And the colors look strange (straynj)? Check the conversion (kuhn·vur·zhn) from RGB to BGR. And if something happens (ha·puhnz) that isn't on this list, do what we did the whole course. Read the error message from the bottom, find the file and the line, and if you can't solve it, send me an email with the full screenshot (skreen·shaat). And one more that happens (ha·puhnz) a lot: the app starts, but nothing (nuh·thuhng) shows in the browser. It's almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) because (buh·kuhz) you opened a different address. The terminal (tur·muh·nl) shows the exact address. Copy it from there."]}, {"n": 61, "part": 1, "title": "Today, in One Slide", "paras": ["Let's close today with four (4) ideas.", "1. First, the detection (duh·tek·shn) page. The model runs once, and the threshold (thresh·howld) only filters. That's the idea that makes the page fast, and keeps the boxes (baak·suhz), the sentence and the table always (aal·wayz) in agreement.", "2. Second, the connected pages. Each page reads what the previous (pree·vee·uhs) one wrote. Nobody copies files by hand. That's why the system works the same on your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) as on your classmate's (klas·mayts).", "3. Third (thurd), the hook. A rule (rool) that always (aal·wayz) runs. And behind that hook is the same old rule (rool). Data and weights (wayts) never go to GitHub (git·huhb).", "4. And fourth, the README (reed·mee). Someone else can run it from nothing (nuh·thuhng). And if you keep only one idea from today, keep this one: a system that only works on your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) isn't a system yet. And that idea will be at the center of Wednesday's class, when we talk about showing the system to someone who has never seen it. And the four (4) support each other. The page uses the threshold (thresh·howld), the connected pages use the folders, the hook protects the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), and the README (reed·mee) explains all of that to another person."]}, {"n": 62, "part": 1, "title": "On Wednesday", "paras": ["On Wednesday (wenz·day), in Class four point two (4.2): how to show your system in three (3) minutes, what it would take to use it for real, what these networks can do besides detecting, and the close of the course. And for Wednesday (wenz·day), if you can, have your system running. Not to show it in class, but because (buh·kuhz) you'll (yool) understand the video part much better if you already have it working.", "1. Both assignments close on Sunday (suhn·day), November (now·vem·br) twenty-second (22). And if you get stuck (stuhk) with any error, send me an email with a screenshot (skreen·shaat) and what you were doing. Thank you very much for your attention, and I'll (ail) see you on Wednesday (wenz·day)."]}, {"n": 63, "part": 2, "title": "Demonstrating It, and Where It Goes Next", "paras": ["Good evening, everyone, and welcome to the second class of Module (maa·jool) four (4), Class four point two (4.2): the last class of the course. On Monday (muhn·day) we put the complete system together. Today we show it. Then we'll look at what it would take to use it outside the classroom, what else these networks can do, and we'll close the course. And today, I want you to think like an outsider (aut·sai·dr). Someone who has never seen your system, and has three (3) minutes to decide (duh·side) whether (weh·thr) it's useful. That point of view is the thread of the whole class. When we talk about the video, the question (kwes·chn) will be: what does someone who has never seen your system understand? When we talk about making it real: what does a city that doesn't know your code need? And when we talk about other fields: what changes when the user is a doctor, or a factory? It's always (aal·wayz) the same question (kwes·chn): what does the other person need? And one more thing before we start. This is the last class, but the week's work goes on until (uhn·tel) Sunday (suhn·day). So everything we see today is meant to be used tomorrow, when you sit down to record your video or finish your README (reed·mee). And something practical for today: have your own system open while we go through (throo) the video part. Many of these ideas make more sense if you picture them on your own page."]}, {"n": 64, "part": 2, "title": "On Monday: One System", "paras": ["Before we start, a quick look back at Monday (muhn·day).", "1. First, the detection (duh·tek·shn) page. The model runs once, and the threshold (thresh·howld) only filters. That idea, running the model once and then only filtering, is what makes the slider respond instantly in your video. And that looks very good on camera (kam·ruh). That page is the one you'll (yool) show most in your video, so today we'll see how to show it well.", "2. Second, the connected pages. And the app tells you what's missing (mi·suhng), and what to do. And that's what makes a system feel friendly. When something is missing (mi·suhng), the person doesn't see a red error they don't understand. They see a sentence that tells them which step is missing (mi·suhng), and how to do it.", "3. Third (thurd), the README (reed·mee) and the limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz). Someone else can run your system from nothing (nuh·thuhng), and knows what it doesn't do. And by now, many of you have already shared your README (reed·mee) in the forum, and you already know where a classmate (klas·mayt) got stuck (stuhk). You'll (yool) use that information (in·fr·may·shn) today. And if your classmate (klas·mayt) already replied in the forum, read the reply before you record. If they got stuck (stuhk) on a step, fix it first. That way your video shows the improved version of the system.", "4. So today, we show it, and we look at where it could go. The first part of today is practical, for your video. The second part is about looking further: what it would take for a city to use it, and what else these networks can do. There's no new code today. It's a class about looking at the system from the outside. And everything we saw on Monday (muhn·day) shows up again today, but from the outside: how it looks to someone who didn't build it."]}, {"n": 65, "part": 2, "title": "Three Minutes, Running", "paras": ["Let's start with the demonstration (deh·muhn·stray·shn). You have about three (3) minutes to show someone your system. How do you do it well?"]}, {"n": 66, "part": 2, "title": "Show It Running, Not Slides About It", "paras": ["The most important (uhm·por·tnt) rule (rool): show the system running, not slides about the system.", "1. What to do. Open the app, and use it. Say what you're doing while you do it. And show a mistake, on purpose (pur·puhs). Why on purpose (pur·puhs)? Because (buh·kuhz) it builds trust. When someone sees that you know the limits of your system, they believe (buh·leev) you about what it can do. And why say what you're doing while you do it? Because (buh·kuhz) the person watching doesn't know where to look. If you say now I move the threshold (thresh·howld) up, they look at the slider, and then they watch the boxes (baak·suhz) disappear. Without that sentence, they just see a screen that changes. And showing the system running has one more advantage (uhd·van·tuhj). It's the proof that it works. A slide can say anything. A system running in front of the person can't be faked.", "2. What not to do. No slides about the app. No reading a script word by word. And no waiting for a training on camera (kam·ruh): that takes an hour. And if something goes wrong (rong) while you record, that's fine. Stop, and record again. That's the advantage (uhd·van·tuhj) of a video over a live presentation (pree·zn·tay·shn). And reading a script word by word really shows. Your voice goes flat, and you lose the rhythm of the clicks. It's better to have a few (fyoo) points written down, and say the rest in your own words. And you don't need to edit the video with effects or music. What's graded is the system and your explanation, not the production. A simple, clear video with good audio is enough (uh·nuhf)."]}, {"n": 67, "part": 2, "title": "A Three-Minute Plan", "paras": ["Here's a plan for your three (3) minutes.", "1. About twenty (20) seconds to say what your system does, and for whom. Then the data page, the training page, and the detection (duh·tek·shn) page, with a little more time for detection (duh·tek·shn). And at the end, about thirty (30) seconds for one mistake. Three (3) minutes go by very fast. If you go over, it's almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) because (buh·kuhz) you explained (uhk·splaynd) how the code works. You don't need to. Show what it does. On the data page: the class chart, two (2) or three (3) photos (fow·towz), and one thing you noticed, like \"there are many small boxes (baak·suhz)\". On the training page: don't explain every curve (kurv). Say how long it took, and whether (weh·thr) it learned (lurnd) fast or slowly. On the detection (duh·tek·shn) page: move the slider, so people can see what changes. And something that helps a lot: practise (prak·tuhs) the detection (duh·tek·shn) part more than the others. It's the new page, the one the person cares about most, and the one where hesitation shows the most. If that part flows, the rest of the video feels confident. And write your opening (ow·puh·nuhng) sentence before you record. For example: my system finds (faindz) potholes (paat·howlz) in street photos (fow·towz), so a crew (kroo) knows where to go first. One sentence like that tells the person why they should keep watching.", "2. Use a run that's already finished, and say why you trained (traynd) it that way. And end with what your system gets wrong (rong). That's what people remember. Why end with the mistake, and not with what works? Because (buh·kuhz) the last thing people see is what they remember. If you end with a mistake that's well explained (uhk·splaynd), the person leaves thinking you know your system. And that's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the impression you want to leave. And a finished run also avoids surprises. If you train during the recording, the run can come out different from the one you know, and the mistake you planned to show may not appear anymore."]}, {"n": 68, "part": 2, "title": "The Same Thirty Seconds, Two Ways", "paras": ["To see the difference (di·fruhns) between a weak demonstration (deh·muhn·stray·shn) and a clear one, let's take the same thirty (30) seconds, told two ways.", "1. This is the weak version. So, this is the training page. Here there are some options. I click here. This comes out. OK, let's go on. Nothing (nuh·thuhng) it says is false (fols). But the person watching learns nothing (nuh·thuhng). They don't know what they're looking at, or why it matters (ma·drz). And almost (aal·mowst) every sentence points to here and this, words that only make sense to someone who already knows where to look. And the weak version usually (yoo·zhoo·uh·lee) comes out when you're nervous. That's why practising once helps. The second time, you already know what to say on each screen.", "2. And this is the clear version. This is the training page. I trained (traynd) on all the photos (fow·towz), because (buh·kuhz) the run with half of them missed (mist) the small potholes (paat·howlz). Here's the finished run, with its reason. It's the same screen, and the same clicks. But each sentence says what it is, and why. And listen for the word because (buh·kuhz). Every time you say it, the person learns something about a decision (duh·si·zhn) you made. In a three-minute video, a few (fyoo) sentences (sen·tn·suhz) with because (buh·kuhz) are worth (wurth) more than many clicks.", "3. Same screen, same clicks. The second one says why. And it isn't longer. Both versions fit in the same time. The difference (di·fruhns) is that the second one was prepared by someone who knew what they wanted to say before pressing record. That's why the three-minute plan is so useful. It isn't a script to read. It's a list of what you want the person to understand on each page. And one practical tip. When you review your video, listen for the words here and this. Every time you say them without saying what it is, the person gets a little lost. Replace them with the name of the thing: the threshold (thresh·howld) slider, the run, the table."]}, {"n": 69, "part": 2, "title": "Choose the Mistake You Show", "paras": ["The end of the video counts the most. So how do you choose the mistake you show? Three (3) rules.", "1. Look at this photo from Module (maa·jool) three (3). It's a good example: potholes (paat·howlz) it found (faund), in green, and potholes (paat·howlz) it missed (mist), in yellow. This photo works as an example because (buh·kuhz) it has all three (3) things at once. What it found (faund), what it missed (mist), and a false (fols) alarm. In thirty (30) seconds, a photo like this tells the whole story of the detector (duh·tek·tr). And in your video, show it on the detection (duh·tek·shn) page, not as a separate (seh·pruht) image. That way the person sees the mistake comes from the running system, not from a prepared photo.", "2. Rule (rool) one: make it real. From your test photos (fow·towz), not one you made up. And real also means you don't set it up to fail. You don't darken the photo, or crop it on purpose (pur·puhs). You look through (throo) your test photos (fow·towz) for one where the detector (duh·tek·tr) got it wrong (rong) on its own. And if it's hard to find a mistake, look first at the test photos (fow·towz) with the most objects (aab·jekts). That's where a detector (duh·tek·tr) usually (yoo·zhoo·uh·lee) fails most.", "3. Rule (rool) two: make it typical (ti·puh·kl). The kind of mistake your detector (duh·tek·tr) makes often, not a rare accident. How do you know which mistake is typical (ti·puh·kl)? From what you saw in Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) three point two (3.2). If it misses small objects (aab·jekts) in many photos (fow·towz), that's your typical (ti·puh·kl) mistake. A mistake that happened only once is a curiosity, not a limitation (li·muh·tay·shn). And if your detector (duh·tek·tr) has several (sev·ruhl) classes, a typical (ti·puh·kl) mistake can be that it confuses two of them. That's a good mistake to show too, because (buh·kuhz) anyone understands the confusion when they see the two classes side by side.", "4. Rule (rool) three: give your why. For example: \"they're shallow (sha·low) and dry, and my guess is that...\". I said \"my guess\". If you didn't test it, say it exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) that way. And the reason doesn't have to be technical (tek·nuh·kl). It can be something you saw in the photos (fow·towz). For example: in my training photos (fow·towz) almost (aal·mowst) every pothole (paat·howl) has water in it, and this one is dry. That observation is worth (wurth) more than a technical (tek·nuh·kl) word you can't check."]}, {"n": 70, "part": 2, "title": "My Own Demonstration", "paras": ["And now, mine, with the pothole (paat·howl) system. Watch how I follow the plan we just saw.", "1. ▶ ON THE PC: I play the three (3) minute video. After the video, I go over out loud (laud) what you just saw: first I said what the system does and for whom, then I went through (throo) the three (3) pages in order, and I ended with a real mistake and my reason. That order is the same one I ask for in your video. And look at what I didn't do. I didn't explain the code, I didn't show slides, and I didn't wait for any training. All the time went into what the system does, and why I made each decision (duh·si·zhn)."]}, {"n": 71, "part": 2, "title": "What Makes a Demonstration Clear", "paras": ["What makes a demonstration (deh·muhn·stray·shn) easy to follow? Four (4) things.", "1. First, make it big. Zoom the browser, and close everything else. People will watch it on a laptop, or on a phone. If it's barely (behr·lee) readable (ree·duh·bl) on your screen, it won't be readable (ree·duh·bl) on theirs. And something almost (aal·mowst) nobody does. Record at a normal resolution (reh·zuh·loo·shn), not the highest one. A huge video takes a long time to upload, and the person watching on a phone doesn't see the difference (di·fruhns). And before recording, set the browser zoom higher than normal. To do it: Control and plus on Windows, Command and plus on a Mac. That way the page text is readable (ree·duh·bl) even on a phone.", "2. Second, one path, no detours. Practise (prak·tuhs) it once before recording. Not to memorize it. Just to know the order of your clicks, so you don't get lost looking for a tab on camera (kam·ruh). And only one browser tab open: your app. If there are more tabs, it's easy to click the wrong (rong) one, and in a three-minute video every lost second shows.", "3. Third (thurd), say the why. Why that data. Why that threshold (thresh·howld). The why is what separates someone who understands their (dhehr) system from someone who just ran it. For example. Saying this is my threshold (thresh·howld) isn't the same as saying I chose this threshold (thresh·howld) because (buh·kuhz) I'd rather have a false (fols) alarm than a pothole (paat·howl) nobody checks. The second sentence shows there was a decision (duh·si·zhn), and that you thought (thaat) about someone. And the why can be simple. I trained (traynd) on all the photos (fow·towz) because (buh·kuhz) with fewer (fyoo·wr), it missed (mist) the small potholes (paat·howlz). One sentence like that is enough (uh·nuhf).", "4. And fourth, show a real mistake, and say why you think it happens (ha·puhnz). And if you don't know why it happens (ha·puhnz), that's fine too. Say my guess is this, and I haven't checked it. That's honest (aa·nuhst), and it's much better than making up an explanation that sounds sure."]}, {"n": 72, "part": 2, "title": "Before You Press Record", "paras": ["Before you press record, a quick check. Six (6) things, and each one saves you from recording everything again.", "1. First, a run that's already finished, selected on the page. No training on camera (kam·ruh). And choose the run you know best, even if it isn't the one with the best number. It's easier to explain a run you understand.", "2. Second, two (2) photos (fow·towz) ready, in a folder that's easy to find. One where the system works well, and one with the mistake you'll (yool) show. If you look for them while you record, you lose time, and you lose the thread. And the mistake you'll (yool) show should be one you already know. This isn't the time to discover a new mistake. It's the time to explain one you already understand.", "3. Third (thurd), notifications off, and other windows closed. A message that pops up in the middle of the video is distracting, and sometimes it shows personal information (in·fr·may·shn). And close your email and chat too. A notification with someone's name, or a private message, shouldn't end up in a video you're going to share (shehr).", "4. Fourth, the browser zoomed in, so the text is easy to read on a small screen.", "5. Fifth, the microphone (mai·kruh·fown) checked. Record ten (10) seconds and listen. Make sure it sounds clear, with no background noise. And if you can, use headphones with a microphone (mai·kruh·fown). The laptop's microphone (mai·kruh·fown) picks up the fan and the keyboard, and that gets tiring for whoever is listening.", "6. And sixth, your three-minute plan next to you, in short points. Not a text to read, just the order. With these six (6) things ready, one or two takes are almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) enough (uh·nuhf). Without them, it's easy to end up recording five or six times. And one trick (trik): have the plan on paper, or on another screen. If it's on the same screen you're recording, the person will see it."]}, {"n": 73, "part": 2, "title": "How to Record Your Screen and Your Voice", "paras": ["So what do you use to record your screen and your voice? Nothing (nuh·thuhng) special to install.", "1. On a Mac: QuickTime Player. File menu, New Screen Recording. When you finish, QuickTime saves the file in a Mac video format, and YouTube and Google Drive accept it with no problem.", "2. On Windows: Clipchamp, which already comes with the system, or the Xbox Game Bar, with the Windows key and G. Clipchamp also lets you cut the beginning and the end, which is where there are almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) a few (fyoo) seconds of silence while you look for the stop button. And on Windows, the Xbox Game Bar sometimes records only the active window. Before recording everything, do a short test and check that the whole browser shows.", "3. And on any computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr): Zoom. Open a meeting with only you, and record it. Zoom is a good option if you already use it for classes. You already know where the record button is, and it records the screen and your voice together. In Zoom, when you end the meeting, the video is saved on your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) or in the cloud, depending on your account. Check where it went before you close everything.", "4. Then share (shehr) it on YouTube as Unlisted, or on Google Drive with access for anyone who has the link. Please don't upload the video file to Moodle (moo·dl): only the link. And before you record everything, do a ten (10) second test and listen to it. Very often the microphone (mai·kruh·fown) is off, and you only find out at the end. And one more thing about YouTube. The first time you upload, it may ask you to verify your account for long videos. Three (3) minutes isn't long, so you shouldn't have that problem. But if something asks you to verify, Google Drive is the simplest alternative."]}, {"n": 74, "part": 2, "title": "Check the Link From Outside", "paras": ["And one step people often forget.", "1. Open your link in a private window, where you're not signed in. In Chrome: Control, Shift and N. On a Mac: Command, Shift and N. In Safari, also Command, Shift and N. This check takes ten (10) seconds, and it prevents the most common (kaa·muhn) problem with this assignment (uh·sain·muhnt). Every term there are videos nobody can open, and the student doesn't know, because (buh·kuhz) from their (dhehr) own account the video plays perfectly (pur·fuhkt·lee). And if you use YouTube, also check that the video has finished processing. Right after uploading, it sometimes plays in low (low) quality (kwaa·luh·dee) for a few (fyoo) minutes.", "2. If it asks you to sign in, or to request access, nobody else can watch it. And I can't grade it. A link that works is part of the grade. And this happens (ha·puhnz) a lot with Google Drive. By default, a file is private. You have to change the access to anyone with the link. On YouTube, the option is Unlisted. Not private, not public. And check that the link you paste in Moodle (moo·dl) is the same one you tested. It happens (ha·puhnz) a lot that people copy the link from the edit page, which only works for the owner, instead of the share (shehr) link. And if you're worried about the video being on YouTube, remember that Unlisted means only people with the link can see it. It doesn't show up in searches."]}, {"n": 75, "part": 2, "title": "Activity: Show It to Someone Who Hasn't Seen It", "paras": ["Now today's activity (ak·ti·vuh·dee). It's the starting point of Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point two (4.2), and it also happens (ha·puhnz) in the forum Run My System.", "1. First, record your system running from start to finish, without explaining as you go. One detail here. Record without explaining. The first time, the video is to see whether (weh·thr) the system runs from start to finish with no problems (praa·bluhmz). The explanations go into the final version. That first recording is for you. Watch it once, and write down where you hesitated (heh·zuh·tay·duhd), or where you lost time. Those are the points to practise (prak·tuhs) before the final version.", "2. Second, by Friday at the latest, post the link in your topic. And in the topic, besides the link, write one sentence about what your system does, and for whom. That sentence is the same one your video starts with.", "3. Third (thurd), by Saturday (sa·tr·day) at the latest, watch a classmate's (klas·mayts) video and tell them what you understood (uhn·dr·stud) their (dhehr) system does. Watch it only once, without pausing, the way an outsider (aut·sai·dr) would. Then write what you understood (uhn·dr·stud). If you understood (uhn·dr·stud) something different from what they meant, that's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) what helps them. And when you reply, say first what you understood (uhn·dr·stud), and then what you didn't. That way your classmate (klas·mayt) knows which part of the video works, and which part needs to change. And don't reply like a teacher. It isn't about grading the video. It's about saying, honestly, what someone watching it for the first time understood (uhn·dr·stud).", "4. And fourth, fix the part that confused (kuhn·fyoozd) your classmate (klas·mayt) the most. And write in the PDF what you changed (chaynjd) thanks to your classmate (klas·mayt). That's the proof that the forum was useful."]}, {"n": 76, "part": 2, "title": "What It Would Take to Make It Real", "paras": ["So your system works in class. Now, what would it take for a city to actually (ak·choo·uh·lee) use it?"]}, {"n": 77, "part": 2, "title": "More Data", "paras": ["The first thing: more data.", "1. Remember this experiment (uhk·speh·ruh·muhnt) from Module (maa·jool) three (3)? Same test photos (fow·towz). The only thing that changed (chaynjd) was how many photos (fow·towz) the model trained (traynd) on. With thirty (30), the detector (duh·tek·tr) fell far behind. With all of them, it did much better. And the interesting (in·truh·stuhng) part is that the model, the code and the test photos (fow·towz) were exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the same. The only thing that changed (chaynjd) was the number of examples. That says a lot about where the real work is in a system like this. And what the experiment (uhk·speh·ruh·muhnt) shows is a trend, not an exact rule (rool). More photos (fow·towz) help a lot at first, and then each new photo helps a little less. But with a few (fyoo) hundred photos (fow·towz), we're still (stil) far from that point.", "2. More photos (fow·towz) helped a lot. And a real system needs thousands (thau·zndz), from many streets and many times of the year. Thousands (thau·zndz) isn't an exaggeration (uhg·za·juh·ray·shn). A pothole (paat·howl) in summer, in winter, wet, dry, by day, by night. Each case needs its own examples. And getting those photos (fow·towz) is the most expensive part of the whole project. Someone has to take them, store them, check them, and label them. That's why, in industry (in·duh·stree), the team that handles the data is often bigger (bi·gr) than the team that handles the model.", "3. And not just any photos (fow·towz). Photos (fow·towz) like the ones it will see at work: the same cameras, the same streets, the same weather (weh·dhr). A dataset (day·tuh·set) of photos (fow·towz) taken with a phone, in daylight, up close, doesn't prepare the model for a camera (kam·ruh) on a truck going by fast. The training photos (fow·towz) have to look like the real situation where it's used."]}, {"n": 78, "part": 2, "title": "Is My mAP Good?", "paras": ["A question (kwes·chn) I get a lot: is my mAP (em·ay·pee) good? The honest (aa·nuhst) answer is that the number alone doesn't tell you. There are three (3) ways to answer it well.", "1. First, compared with what? A number makes sense next to another number, measured the same way. For example, with the same test photos (fow·towz) and another run, like the experiment (uhk·speh·ruh·muhnt) we just saw. Comparing your number with one from a paper that used other photos (fow·towz) says almost (aal·mowst) nothing (nuh·thuhng), because (buh·kuhz) hard photos (fow·towz) change everything. And if you don't have another run to compare with, compare with something simple. For example, with what a person saw in those same photos (fow·towz). That's an honest (aa·nuhst) comparison too.", "2. Second, look at the photos (fow·towz). Two (2) detectors with the same mAP (em·ay·pee) can fail on very different things. One can miss the small potholes (paat·howlz), and the other the ones in shadow (sha·dow). The number is the same, but for the crew (kroo) it isn't the same thing. For example, in our (aw·r) system, the number doesn't say that shallow (sha·low) potholes (paat·howlz) slip through (throo). We only saw that by looking at the photos (fow·towz). Two systems with the same number can need completely different improvements.", "3. And third (thurd), good for whom? A detector (duh·tek·tr) that helps a crew (kroo) sort photos (fow·towz) can be useful with a modest number, because (buh·kuhz) a person checks everything afterwards. A system that decides alone, with nobody checking, needs much more. So when you write your results, don't just give the number. Say what you compare it with, what mistakes (muh·stayks) it makes, and what use it's good enough (uh·nuhf) for. And in your README (reed·mee), that sentence can be very short. For example: good enough (uh·nuhf) to sort photos (fow·towz) a person will check, not to decide (duh·side) alone."]}, {"n": 79, "part": 2, "title": "Better Labels", "paras": ["The second thing: better labels (lay·buhlz).", "1. One rule (rool) for every box (baaks). Where does a pothole (paat·howl) end? Does a crack count? Write it down, and make everyone who labels (lay·buhlz) follow it. Why? If two (2) people label with different rules, the model learns both rules at once, and gets confused (kuhn·fyoozd). And so does the metric, because (buh·kuhz) the test photos (fow·towz) have the same problem. In industry (in·duh·stree), that rule (rool) is written in a document called an annotation guide. It has examples of what counts and what doesn't, with photos (fow·towz). And every new person who labels (lay·buhlz) reads it before they start. And a simple way to know whether (weh·thr) the rule (rool) is clear: ask two people to label the same ten (10) photos (fow·towz) without talking to each other. If the boxes (baak·suhz) look alike, the rule (rool) is clear. If not, it has to be written better before labeling a thousand more photos (fow·towz). And in our (aw·r) dataset (day·tuh·set) we found (faund) a real example: one box (baaks) that went outside the image, and several (sev·ruhl) very small boxes (baak·suhz). With an annotation guide, those cases get checked before training.", "2. And the small ones. Many of our (aw·r) boxes (baak·suhz) are very small. If small potholes (paat·howlz) are left unlabeled, the model learns to ignore them. And the problem is double. In training, the model learns that those areas aren't potholes (paat·howlz). And in testing, when the model does find them, it counts as a false (fols) alarm, because (buh·kuhz) there was no label. The number punishes the model for being right. And the fix isn't hard, but it takes time: go back to the photos (fow·towz) and check for potholes (paat·howlz) without a label. It's boring, but it's one of the improvements that helps a detector (duh·tek·tr) the most."]}, {"n": 80, "part": 2, "title": "The Cases It Should Refuse", "paras": ["The third (thurd) thing: the cases the system should refuse.", "1. Look at this. A detector (duh·tek·tr) trained (traynd) on COCO, the same starting point as yours, on a video taken with a drone. It found (faund) zero cars. Not one. And it isn't that the video is bad. You can see many cars, clear and well lit. A person would count them easily. The problem is the angle. And it's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the kind of case nobody thinks to test, because (buh·kuhz) it never appeared in training. That's why it's a good idea to test the system on purpose (pur·puhs) with strange (straynj) photos (fow·towz), before a user does. And the model has no way of knowing it's outside of what it knows. It always (aal·wayz) gives (givz) an answer. That's why that check has to be in the system, not in the model.", "2. Why? It learned (lurnd) what cars look like from the side, not from above (uh·buhv). And here's the dangerous (dayn·jr·uhs) part: it doesn't warn you. It just says \"nothing (nuh·thuhng) here\". A real system should say \"I can't judge this photo\", not \"there's nothing (nuh·thuhng)\". How? There are several (sev·ruhl) ways. A simple one: check whether (weh·thr) the photo looks like the training photos (fow·towz), for example in its light or its angle, and warn when it doesn't. And the same thing happens (ha·puhnz) in your own project. If you trained (traynd) on photos (fow·towz) taken from the front, a photo taken from above (uh·buhv), or from very far away, can give zero detections (duh·tek·shnz), and the page will say it found (faund) nothing (nuh·thuhng). To the user, that looks like a result. In reality, it's the system outside of what it knows. And for your README (reed·mee), this becomes one simple sentence: what kind of photos (fow·towz) the system is for. For example: daytime photos (fow·towz), taken from the front, at a person's height. Outside of that, the user knows the result isn't reliable."]}, {"n": 81, "part": 2, "title": "Stress-Test It Before Someone Else Does", "paras": ["Here's a way to find limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz) before someone else does: put your detector (duh·tek·tr) under pressure.", "1. Same test photo, darker each time. With the normal photo, it finds (faindz) several (sev·ruhl) potholes (paat·howlz). The darker it gets, the fewer (fyoo·wr) it finds (faindz). And this test is very easy to do. It doesn't need new photos (fow·towz) or new labels (lay·buhlz). You just take photos (fow·towz) you already have, change them a little, and see what happens (ha·puhnz). You can do the same with photos (fow·towz) that are blurrier, smaller, or rotated. And the conclusion (kuhn·kloo·zhn) isn't that the detector (duh·tek·tr) is bad. It's that we know its limits a little better. Before the test, we didn't know what happened in low (low) light. Now we have a clue.", "2. But careful with the conclusion (kuhn·kloo·zhn). A darkened photo isn't a real night. At night there are car lights, shadows, and noise. Still (stil), it tells you where to look first. And to really know how it works at night, you need real night photos (fow·towz). And this goes straight into your README (reed·mee). If darkness makes it find fewer (fyoo·wr), write it as a limitation (li·muh·tay·shn), with the image as evidence (eh·vuh·duhns), and say you didn't test it with real night photos (fow·towz). And if you want to use this test in your extension (uhk·sten·shn), the idea is the same with real photos (fow·towz). Take a few (fyoo) photos (fow·towz) at night or in the rain, label them, and compare with your test set. That turns a suspicion into a measurement."]}, {"n": 82, "part": 2, "title": "Running Somewhere Other Than a Laptop", "paras": ["And the fourth thing: running somewhere other than a laptop. Three (3) typical (ti·puh·kl) options.", "1. First, in a vehicle (vee·uh·kl), with a camera (kam·ruh) that checks the street as it drives. This already exists. Some cities put cameras on garbage trucks or buses, which drive the same streets every day. And in a vehicle (vee·uh·kl), a new problem shows up. The camera (kam·ruh) moves. Photos (fow·towz) come out blurry, with reflections, with rain on the glass. Those are all cases our (aw·r) dataset (day·tuh·set) doesn't have.", "2. Second, on a server, where many crews send their (dhehr) photos (fow·towz). The app you built is almost (aal·mowst) ready for that. Gradio (gray·dee·ow) can be opened to other people on a network. What's missing (mi·suhng) is everything else: users, security (suh·kyur·uh·dee), and not crashing. And on a server, another question (kwes·chn) appears: what happens (ha·puhnz) to the photos (fow·towz) that arrive? Are they kept? For how long? Who can see them? Those are questions our (aw·r) system never had to answer, because (buh·kuhz) it ran on one computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr). Gradio (gray·dee·ow) even has an option to create a temporary link another person can open. It's good for a quick demo, but not for a real system, because (buh·kuhz) the link doesn't last long, and it depends on your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) being on.", "3. And third (thurd), on a small device: cheap, slow, with no graphics (gra·fiks) card. Each option changes the problem. In a vehicle (vee·uh·kl), speed matters (ma·drz). On a server, how many photos (fow·towz) arrive at once. On a small device, memory. And on a small device, sometimes you need an even smaller model, even if it detects a little worse (wurs). It's the same trade again: how well, against how fast, and with how much memory. And there's no right option for everyone. It depends on who uses it, how many photos (fow·towz) there are, and how much money there is. That's another design decision (duh·si·zhn), just like the threshold (thresh·howld)."]}, {"n": 83, "part": 2, "title": "Export the Model: ONNX", "paras": ["For that, you usually (yoo·zhoo·uh·lee) export the model to another format. The most common (kaa·muhn) one is called ONNX.", "1. Look at the table. The same pothole (paat·howl) detector (duh·tek·tr), on the hundred (100) test photos (fow·towz), on my Mac. With ONNX, each photo took quite a bit less time. So what's ONNX? A standard format for saving a model, one that doesn't depend on the library (lai·breh·ree) you trained (traynd) it with. We trained (traynd) with PyTorch (pai·torch), but another program can run it, on another system, even on a phone. And to compare fairly, we used exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the same photos (fow·towz) with both formats. If the photos (fow·towz) change, the comparison doesn't tell you anything anymore.", "2. And it's just two (2) lines of code. Load the model. Export it. The image size line is important (uhm·por·tnt). The exported model expects photos (fow·towz) of that size, the same as the model you trained (traynd). If you change it, the results change. And after exporting, it's a good idea to check that the exported model gives (givz) the same results as the original on a few (fyoo) photos (fow·towz). If the boxes (baak·suhz) change a lot, something went wrong (rong) in the export.", "3. Here it was about twice as fast, and it runs without PyTorch (pai·torch), which is a very heavy library (lai·breh·ree). The ONNX file is a bit bigger (bi·gr), but faster here. And that's the lesson: always (aal·wayz) measure (meh·zhr) on the machine where it will run. Results change from one computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) to another. And ONNX isn't the only option. There are other formats made for phones, or for specific (spuh·si·fik) graphics (gra·fiks) cards. Ultralytics (uhl·truh·li·tiks) can export to several (sev·ruhl) of them with the same line, changing only the format name. The idea is always (aal·wayz) the same: the trained (traynd) model leaves its training environment (uhn·vai·rn·muhnt), and gets ready for the place where it will work."]}, {"n": 84, "part": 2, "title": "Measure It on Your Own Computer", "paras": ["We saw the exported model was faster on my Mac. But we also said speed depends on the machine. So how do you measure (meh·zhr) it on your computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr)? With a few (fyoo) lines.", "1. This is the code. It loads the model, takes a list of your test photos (fow·towz), runs the model once on the first photo, and then measures how long it takes on average (av·ruhj) for all the rest. At the end, it prints the seconds per photo. You can run it once with best.pt and once with best.onnx, and compare. If it asks you to install a package for ONNX, that's normal. Ultralytics (uhl·truh·li·tiks) needs an extra one to run that format. And it doesn't need many photos (fow·towz). The ones in your test set are enough (uh·nuhf). What matters (ma·drz) is that there are more than just a few (fyoo), so the average (av·ruhj) doesn't depend on one photo. And you can ask Claude (klaad) to explain each line before you run it. It's a short script, and understanding it helps you measure (meh·zhr) any model later.", "2. Why run one photo first without measuring (meh·zhr·uhng) it? Because (buh·kuhz) the first time, the program loads the model and gets things ready in memory. That first photo is always (aal·wayz) slow, and if you count it, the average (av·ruhj) comes out worse (wurs) than it really is.", "3. And the most important (uhm·por·tnt) part: the same photos (fow·towz) for both models. If one runs on big photos (fow·towz) and the other on small ones, the comparison means nothing (nuh·thuhng). And if you want to use this in your extension (uhk·sten·shn), write the result like this: on my computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr), with these photos (fow·towz), this model took this long. That makes it clear it's your measurement, not a promise (praa·muhs) for every machine. And also write down which computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) you used: the processor, the memory, and whether (weh·thr) it has a graphics (gra·fiks) card. Without that, the number can't be compared with anything."]}, {"n": 85, "part": 2, "title": "Fast Enough for What?", "paras": ["But how fast is fast enough (uh·nuhf)?", "1. Tracking people and cars in a video, on our (aw·r) laptop, takes almost (aal·mowst) half a second per frame. That's about two (2) frames per second. And that's on a laptop with no graphics (gra·fiks) card. With a graphics (gra·fiks) card, the same model can run many times faster. And it's a small model. A bigger (bi·gr) model would be more accurate, but even slower. There's always (aal·wayz) a trade between how well it detects, and how fast it answers. And it was measured on the same laptop we used for the whole course, with nothing (nuh·thuhng) special. It's a good reminder that speed numbers (nuhm·brz) always (aal·wayz) depend on the machine.", "2. A video has thirty (30) frames per second. For a crew (kroo) reviewing (ree·vyoo·uhng) photos (fow·towz), two (2) per second is plenty. For a car driving at sixty (60) kilometers (kuh·laa·muh·trz) per hour, it isn't. The use decides what \"fast enough (uh·nuhf)\" means. It's the same question (kwes·chn) as \"who pays for each mistake\", but with time: fast for whom, and for which decision (duh·si·zhn)? And look at the sentence on the slide. It doesn't say our (aw·r) system is slow. It says it's slow for one thing, and enough (uh·nuhf) for another. That's how you should write about speed in your README (reed·mee): always (aal·wayz) next to the use. And you don't always (aal·wayz) need to check every frame. If the camera (kam·ruh) moves slowly, checking a few (fyoo) frames per second can be enough (uh·nuhf), because (buh·kuhz) a pothole (paat·howl) shows up in many frames in a row."]}, {"n": 86, "part": 2, "title": "Break 2 · 5 Minutes", "paras": ["Let's take the second break: five (5) minutes. When we come back, systems like ours, and what else these networks can do."]}, {"n": 87, "part": 2, "title": "Comparable Systems", "paras": ["All right, let's continue. What does a system like ours look like outside the classroom?"]}, {"n": 88, "part": 2, "title": "Road Inspection, Outside the Classroom", "paras": ["Road inspection (uhn·spek·shn) with computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr) vision (vi·zhn) already exists. And three (3) things are different there.", "1. First, cameras on vehicles (vee·uh·klz), taking photos (fow·towz) every few (fyoo) meters (mee·trz), with their (dhehr) location. With the location, each photo becomes a point on a map (em·ay·pee). The city doesn't get a list of photos (fow·towz). It gets a map (em·ay·pee) of where the damage (da·muhj) is. And the photos (fow·towz) are taken without anyone having to stop. The vehicle (vee·uh·kl) does its normal route, and the camera (kam·ruh) works on its own. That's what makes checking a whole city possible.", "2. Second, big shared datasets (day·tuh·sets), with road photos (fow·towz) from several (sev·ruhl) countries. And those datasets (day·tuh·sets) exist, and they're public. On the next slide I'll (ail) show you a real one. And sharing data has a huge advantage (uhd·van·tuhj). A small team, with no budget to take thousands (thau·zndz) of photos (fow·towz), can start with a public dataset (day·tuh·set), and then add photos (fow·towz) from its own city.", "3. And third (thurd), a person decides. The model sorts the photos (fow·towz), and a crew (kroo) confirms. Even in industry (in·duh·stree), the model almost (aal·mowst) never decides alone. It organizes people's work. That's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) what our (aw·r) system does with the threshold (thresh·howld). And that changes how the threshold (thresh·howld) is chosen. If a person checks everything, you can accept some false (fols) alarms. If nobody checks, every false (fols) alarm is a truck that goes out for nothing (nuh·thuhng). And if the person disagrees with the model, that correction is valuable information (in·fr·may·shn). Many systems keep those corrections to use them as new labels (lay·buhlz)."]}, {"n": 89, "part": 2, "title": "A Real Benchmark: RDD2022", "paras": ["I said big shared road datasets (day·tuh·sets) exist. This is a real one, and it's useful to compare it with ours. It's called RDD twenty twenty-two (2022), and it was published together with an open road damage (da·muhj) detection (duh·tek·shn) challenge.", "1. First, the photos (fow·towz) come from six (6) countries: Japan, India, the Czech Republic, Norway, the United States and China. There are more than forty-seven thousand (47,000). And that matters (ma·drz) a lot, because (buh·kuhz) a road in Norway doesn't look like a road in India. Different asphalt (as·fawlt), different light, different weather (weh·dhr). A detector (duh·tek·tr) trained (traynd) on all of them learns something more general. And thinking about that helps you understand your own results. If your detector (duh·tek·tr) was trained (traynd) with photos (fow·towz) from one place, it may work well there, and worse (wurs) somewhere else. That isn't a bug in the code. It's what the detector (duh·tek·tr) could learn from what it saw.", "2. Second, it doesn't have just one class. It has four (4) kinds of damage (da·muhj): three (3) kinds of cracks, and potholes (paat·howlz). And the photos (fow·towz) were taken from vehicles (vee·uh·klz), which is exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) the real use case we talked about. Our (aw·r) system, on the other hand, has one class, and a few (fyoo) hundred photos (fow·towz). And having several (sev·ruhl) kinds of damage (da·muhj) changes the problem. A long, thin crack doesn't look like a pothole (paat·howl). The detector (duh·tek·tr) has to learn very different shapes, and the boxes (baak·suhz) for a long crack are very stretched, which also makes IoU (ai·ow·yoo) harder to measure (meh·zhr).", "3. But the idea is the same as yours. Photos (fow·towz), labels (lay·buhlz) with boxes (baak·suhz), a detector (duh·tek·tr), and a measurement on a test set. The difference (di·fruhns) isn't the method. It's the scale, and the variety of roads. And if any of you wants to keep going with this project after the course, this dataset (day·tuh·set) is a very good next step. You can use the same code from the course. And you can also use it to test your own detector (duh·tek·tr). If you test it with photos (fow·towz) from another country, you'll (yool) see right away how general what it learned (lurnd) is."]}, {"n": 90, "part": 2, "title": "What They Have That We Don't", "paras": ["So what do they have that we don't?", "1. Data at scale, and people paid to label it. Graphics (gra·fiks) cards, to train many versions and compare them. And monitoring (maa·nuh·tr·uhng): they keep checking the model after they start using it, because (buh·kuhz) roads, cameras and seasons change. And here's something you rarely hear. In a real system, a big part of the work isn't the model at all. It's everything around it: the data, the labels (lay·buhlz), the app, the tests, the monitoring (maa·nuh·tr·uhng). Exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) what you did this month. And something else they have: time. A team can spend months on one single improvement (uhm·proov·muhnt), testing, measuring (meh·zhr·uhng), and testing again. You had four (4) weeks for the whole system. That's why what you built is a serious prototype, not a finished product, and it's fine for the README (reed·mee) to say so. And they also have processes. Before changing the model in use, they test it with new photos (fow·towz), compare it with the previous (pree·vee·uhs) one, and someone approves the change. It's the same idea as our (aw·r) plan before the code, at another scale.", "2. And what you already have is the method. A question (kwes·chn), data, a test set, a threshold (thresh·howld), and honesty (aa·nuh·stee) about the limits. That doesn't change with scale. And that method is what lets you work on a big team. When you join (joyn) a project like that, you'll (yool) recognize the same pieces: the specification (speh·suh·fuh·kay·shn), the tests, the threshold (thresh·howld), the README (reed·mee), and the limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz) section. Just bigger (bi·gr)."]}, {"n": 91, "part": 2, "title": "Street Photos Have People in Them", "paras": ["There's a topic that almost (aal·mowst) never comes up when you train a detector (duh·tek·tr), but comes up right away when a city uses it: street photos (fow·towz) have people in them.", "1. First, faces. A camera (kam·ruh) that drives down the street every day photographs the same people, in the same places, at the same times. With enough (uh·nuhf) photos (fow·towz), you could learn someone's routine.", "2. Second, license plates. Every parked car gets recorded, with its location and its time. And license plates are especially (uh·speh·shuh·lee) sensitive data, because (buh·kuhz) they lead straight to a person. A pothole (paat·howl) system doesn't need to read them or keep them.", "3. And third (thurd), what big services already do. Google Street View, for example, automatically blurs faces and license plates before it publishes its photos (fow·towz). And that's done with a detector (duh·tek·tr) too. One model finds (faindz) the faces and the plates, and another step blurs them. And here's what's interesting (in·truh·stuhng) about this. The same technique you learned (lurnd) in this course can be used to protect people. A detector (duh·tek·tr) doesn't only find potholes (paat·howlz). It can also find what shouldn't be kept.", "4. A pothole (paat·howl) system doesn't need to know who was there. So the rule (rool) is simple: keep only what the system needs. The pothole (paat·howl) boxes (baak·suhz) and the location, yes. The full photo with people in it, only if it's needed, and blurred. And think about this for your own project too. If your photos (fow·towz) have people or private data in them, that's a good reason never to upload them to a public repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), even if there are only a few (fyoo). And in many countries there are data protection (pruh·tek·shn) laws that require this. A city can't keep photos (fow·towz) of people without a reason, and without clear rules. A well-designed system takes this into account from the start."]}, {"n": 92, "part": 2, "title": "After It Is Deployed: Keep Measuring", "paras": ["And when the system is in real use, is the work over? No.", "1. First, keep a sample: a few (fyoo) photos (fow·towz) from real use, every month. You don't need to keep everything. A few (fyoo) random photos (fow·towz) are enough (uh·nuhf) to see whether (weh·thr) something changed (chaynjd). And keeping that sample has to be part of the system from the start. If nobody planned it, later there are no photos (fow·towz) to compare, and no way to know whether (weh·thr) the model got worse (wurs).", "2. Second, label some of them, with the same rules as your dataset (day·tuh·set). And this is where the annotation guide matters (ma·drz) again. If the new photos (fow·towz) are labeled (lay·buhld) with a different rule (rool), the comparison doesn't work. You'd be measuring (meh·zhr·uhng) the change in the rule (rool), not the change in the model. And you don't need to label many. A few (fyoo) dozen, labeled (lay·buhld) well, already show whether (weh·thr) something changed (chaynjd) in an important (uhm·por·tnt) way.", "3. Third (thurd), compare. Calculate (kal·kyuh·layt) precision (pruh·si·zhn) and recall, and compare them with your test results. If precision (pruh·si·zhn) or recall drop a lot, it's a sign the world changed (chaynjd), and the model didn't. Then it's time to retrain, with new photos (fow·towz) that look like the situation now. And compare with the same threshold (thresh·howld). If the threshold (thresh·howld) changed (chaynjd), precision (pruh·si·zhn) and recall change, even if the model is the same.", "4. A new camera (kam·ruh), winter, another city. The numbers (nuhm·brz) move, and nobody tells you. The only way to know is to keep measuring (meh·zhr·uhng). This has a name in industry (in·duh·stree): the world changes, and the training data gets old. No model stays good forever without someone checking it."]}, {"n": 93, "part": 2, "title": "Beyond Detection", "paras": ["This course was about detection (duh·tek·shn). But the same convolutional (kaan·vuh·loo·shn·uhl) networks can do much more. Let's see what."]}, {"n": 94, "part": 2, "title": "Four Questions About an Image", "paras": ["Think of four (4) questions you can ask about an image.", "1. First, classification (kla·suh·fuh·kay·shn): what's in this photo? That's the simplest question (kwes·chn). The answer is one label for the whole photo. There's a pothole (paat·howl), or there isn't. But it doesn't say where, or how many. And for some problems (praa·bluhmz), classification (kla·suh·fuh·kay·shn) is enough (uh·nuhf). If the crew (kroo) only needs to know which photos (fow·towz) to check, a network that says damage (da·muhj) or no damage (da·muhj) already organizes the work, and it's easier to train.", "2. Second, detection (duh·tek·shn), what we did in this course: where is each object? The answer is a box (baaks). And that's the one we used for our (aw·r) system, because (buh·kuhz) the crew (kroo) needs to know where each pothole (paat·howl) is, and how many there are in a photo. And detection (duh·tek·shn) also answers the first question (kwes·chn). If it finds (faindz) boxes (baak·suhz), we already know what's in the photo. That's why detection (duh·tek·shn) is one step beyond classification (kla·suh·fuh·kay·shn).", "3. Third (thurd), segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn): which pixels belong to the object? The answer isn't a box (baaks) anymore. It's a mask: an image where each pixel says whether (weh·thr) it belongs to the object or not.", "4. And fourth, tracking: is it the same object in the next frame of the video? And each question (kwes·chn) builds on the one before. To track an object, you first have to detect it. To segment it, the network has to know where it is. So everything you learned (lurnd) works for all four (4). And the question (kwes·chn) you choose depends on the user, again. The crew (kroo) needs where and how many, so detection (duh·tek·shn). If they needed the exact size, it would be segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn). If the photos (fow·towz) came (kaym) from a video, you'd need tracking, so the same pothole (paat·howl) isn't counted many times."]}, {"n": 95, "part": 2, "title": "Segmentation: Boxes or Pixels", "paras": ["Here's the difference (di·fruhns) between detection (duh·tek·shn) and segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn), on the cover (kuh·vr) photo.", "1. First, detection (duh·tek·shn): boxes (baak·suhz) around the people and the truck. And the boxes (baak·suhz) overlap. When two people stand close together, their (dhehr) boxes (baak·suhz) cover (kuh·vr) each other. That makes it hard to know where one ends and the other begins.", "2. Second, segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn): the exact shape of each one, pixel by pixel. The mask follows the outline of the truck, and of each person. Not a rectangle (rek·tang·gl). And the labels (lay·buhlz) change too. To train segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn), drawing a box (baaks) isn't enough (uh·nuhf). You have to draw the outline of each object, point by point. That takes much longer per photo. And look at the people standing together. With boxes (baak·suhz), they blend. With masks, each one has its own shape, even if they're touching.", "3. Same photo, same family of models, and segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn) takes only a little longer. So if you need the exact size of something, you need segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn). For example: how much asphalt (as·fawlt) does the crew (kroo) need? A box (baaks) can't tell you, because (buh·kuhz) a pothole (paat·howl) isn't a rectangle (rek·tang·gl). A mask can. And be careful with one thing. A size in pixels isn't a real size. A pothole (paat·howl) close to the camera (kam·ruh) takes up many pixels, and one far away takes up few (fyoo), even if they're the same size. To go from pixels to meters (mee·trz), you need to know where the camera (kam·ruh) is. We'll come back to that in a moment, with Claude (klaad). And Claude (klaad), in the session we'll see in a moment, pointed out something more. Asphalt (as·fawlt) is a volume, not an area (eh·ree·uh). To know how much material to bring, you'd also need the depth of the pothole (paat·howl), and a normal photo doesn't give you that."]}, {"n": 96, "part": 2, "title": "Tracking: the Same One, Frame After Frame", "paras": ["And now tracking, on a video of a street crossing.", "1. First, the first frame. Every person and every car gets a number. That number doesn't mean anything special. It's just a name, so each one can be followed.", "2. Second, a few (fyoo) seconds later. The numbers (nuhm·brz) stay with them as they move. So how does it know it's the same one? In every frame, it detects everything again. Then it compares the new boxes (baak·suhz) with the ones from the previous (pree·vee·uhs) frame. If a new box (baaks) overlaps a lot with an old one, it gets the same number. And how does it measure (meh·zhr) the overlap? With IoU (ai·ow·yoo), the same one from Module (maa·jool) three (3). That idea, comparing with the previous (pree·vee·uhs) frame, is simple, and it works surprisingly well when objects (aab·jekts) move slowly. When they move very fast, or cross each other, it starts to fail, and that's why more elaborate methods exist. And when two people cross paths, their (dhehr) boxes (baak·suhz) overlap a lot for a moment. That's exactly (uhg·zakt·lee) when tracking can swap their (dhehr) numbers (nuhm·brz).", "3. Third (thurd), the end of the clip. Some numbers (nuhm·brz) are gone, because (buh·kuhz) those people left the image. And new numbers (nuhm·brz) appeared, for people who came (kaym) in.", "4. Every object keeps its number while it moves. With that, you can count how many people cross, or how many cars stop. It's detection (duh·tek·shn), plus memory. But look for the mistakes (muh·stayks) too. Sometimes an object loses its number when someone walks in front of it, and comes back with a new one. Same lesson as always (aal·wayz): look at the images (i·muh·juhz). And the tracking used the same kind of detector (duh·tek·tr) you know, trained (traynd) on COCO. Nothing (nuh·thuhng) new had to be trained (traynd). Only the memory between frames was added."]}, {"n": 97, "part": 2, "title": "Counting Without Counting Twice", "paras": ["I said that with tracking, you can count. But counting with video has a trap, and it's worth (wurth) looking at calmly.", "1. These are the three (3) steps. First, detect in every frame of the video, like any detector (duh·tek·tr) does. Second, keep each object's number from one frame to the next, which is what tracking does. And third (thurd), count each number only once. Often, a line is drawn (draan) on the image, and each number is counted when it crosses the line. That way, a person walking through (throo) the scene is counted once, even if they appear in many frames. And the line doesn't have to be a real line on the street. It's a line you draw on the image, wherever (weh·reh·vr) you want to count. For example, the entrance of a parking lot, or the edge of a crosswalk.", "2. And what happens (ha·puhnz) without tracking? A car seen in thirty (30) frames gets counted thirty times. The detector (duh·tek·tr) doesn't know it's the same car. For the detector (duh·tek·tr), every frame is a new photo. And be careful with the mistake we saw earlier. If an object loses its number when someone walks in front of it, and comes back with a new number, it gets counted twice. That's why a system that counts has to be measured too. You count by hand on a few (fyoo) videos, and compare with what the system counted. It's the same idea as always (aal·wayz): don't believe (buh·leev) the number without looking. And to measure (meh·zhr) the count, you don't need boxes (baak·suhz). It's enough (uh·nuhf) to count by hand how many people cross in a few (fyoo) short videos. It's a cheap way to know whether (weh·thr) the system counts well."]}, {"n": 98, "part": 2, "title": "Medical Imaging", "paras": ["Another very important (uhm·por·tnt) application (a·pluh·kay·shn): medical (meh·duh·kl) imaging (i·muh·juhng).", "1. The same kind of networks look for nodules (naa·joolz), fractures (frak·chrz), or cells. Often with the same architectures (aar·kuh·tek·chrz) we used. What changes is the data, and who labels (lay·buhlz) it. And one important (uhm·por·tnt) difference (di·fruhns). In an X-ray, the thing you're looking for can be very small and very subtle. Sometimes a specialist takes years to learn to see it. And medical (meh·duh·kl) data has one extra difficulty: privacy. Patient images (i·muh·juhz) can't be shared freely, so public datasets (day·tuh·sets) are smaller, and every hospital has its own rules. And very often you start from a model trained (traynd) on everyday photos (fow·towz), just like you started from COCO. What it learned (lurnd) about edges and shapes still (stil) helps, even if the images (i·muh·juhz) are very different.", "2. And three (3) things change. A missed (mist) case can cost a life. The labels (lay·buhlz) must come from specialists. And the law asks for proof before it's used. Remember the threshold (thresh·howld) question (kwes·chn), who pays for each mistake? Here, it becomes the most important (uhm·por·tnt) question (kwes·chn). And usually (yoo·zhoo·uh·lee) the answer is a low (low) threshold (thresh·howld), one that finds (faindz) almost (aal·mowst) everything, even with false (fols) alarms. Why? Because (buh·kuhz) a doctor will check a false (fols) alarm. Nobody checks the case that was missed (mist). And one more important (uhm·por·tnt) difference (di·fruhns). In medicine, the system almost (aal·mowst) never replaces the doctor. It helps them look at the most suspicious images (i·muh·juhz) first, or marks areas they could miss. Just like the crew (kroo) with the potholes (paat·howlz): the model organizes the work, and the person decides. And in medicine, recall gets a lot of attention, because (buh·kuhz) a missed (mist) case is the worst outcome. A system can have modest precision (pruh·si·zhn) and still (stil) be useful, if it misses almost (aal·mowst) nothing (nuh·thuhng)."]}, {"n": 99, "part": 2, "title": "Quality Inspection", "paras": ["And one more: quality (kwaa·luh·dee) inspection (uhn·spek·shn) in factories.", "1. A camera (kam·ruh) over the production line looks for scratches, missing (mi·suhng) parts, or bad welds. And here speed matters (ma·drz) a lot. The line doesn't stop to wait for the model. If the camera (kam·ruh) is slow, the part has already gone by. And that camera (kam·ruh) doesn't get tired. A person checking parts for eight (8) hours starts to miss things at the end of the shift. The camera (kam·ruh) checks the first part the same way as the last one. And the camera (kam·ruh) is always (aal·wayz) in the same place, with the same light. That's why a system like that can work very well with relatively few (fyoo) photos (fow·towz).", "2. In one way it's easier than the street: the light and the angle never change. But there's a catch. Defects are rare, so you have very few (fyoo) examples of what's wrong (rong). So what do you do? One idea: train on what's normal, and flag anything that looks different. It's another kind of model, but the question (kwes·chn) underneath is the same. And here, who pays for each mistake changes too. A bad part that gets through (throo) can end up in a car. A good part that gets rejected only costs money. That's why, in many factories, the threshold (thresh·howld) is set so that almost (aal·mowst) nothing (nuh·thuhng) bad gets through (throo), even if some good parts are rejected. And in a factory, it's easy to get many photos (fow·towz) of good parts. That's why the idea works: the model learns very well what normal looks like, and anything different catches its attention."]}, {"n": 100, "part": 2, "title": "Ask Claude: What Would It Take?", "paras": ["To close this part, I asked Claude (klaad) a question (kwes·chn) a real crew (kroo) could ask. A real session, and it took a few (fyoo) seconds.", "1. First, the question (kwes·chn): can my detector (duh·tek·tr) also tell how big each pothole (paat·howl) is, in square meters (mee·trz)? Without writing code. I explicitly asked it not to write code. For a question (kwes·chn) like this, you first need to understand what would change. The code comes later, if someone decides it's worth (wurth) it.", "2. Second, its answer. A box (baaks) is the wrong (rong) shape: you'd need segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn) masks, like the ones we just saw. So you'd switch to a segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn) model. And to go from pixels to meters (mee·trz), you'd need a fixed, calibrated (ka·luh·bray·duhd) camera (kam·ruh). Then it ended with a question (kwes·chn): would the crew (kroo) accept small, medium and large, or do they really need square meters (mee·trz)? And look at what it didn't do. No code, because (buh·kuhz) I asked for none. And no \"it's easy\". It explained (uhk·splaynd) what changes in each part of the system. And it named the three (3) parts of the system that would change: the labels (lay·buhlz), the model, and the camera (kam·ruh). That's the right way to think about a change. Not only which model to use, but what changes in the data, and in the real world. And it pointed out a problem our (aw·r) own system hides. In our (aw·r) Module (maa·jool) three (3) experiment (uhk·speh·ruh·muhnt), large meant large in pixels. A small pothole (paat·howl) close to the camera (kam·ruh) counts as large, and a big one far away counts as small. It also recommended measuring (meh·zhr·uhng) a few (fyoo) potholes (paat·howlz) by hand, with a tape, to compare.", "3. It ended with a question (kwes·chn) about the user, not about the model. That's the right question (kwes·chn). And that's a lesson for your extension (uhk·sten·shn). Before thinking about the model, think about what the user really needs. Often, a simpler answer, like small, medium or large, works just as well, and is much easier to build."]}, {"n": 101, "part": 2, "title": "Closing the Course", "paras": ["And that brings (bringz) us to the close of the course."]}, {"n": 102, "part": 2, "title": "What You Built in Four Weeks", "paras": ["Look at everything you built in four (4) weeks.", "1. Module (maa·jool) one (1): kernels (kur·nlz) written by hand, in src/classical.py. Remember writing a filter by hand, number by number? That was only four (4) weeks ago. That filter written by hand is the same idea that's inside every layer of the network you used later. The network just learns the numbers (nuhm·brz) of the filter, instead of you writing them. And that module (maa·jool) is where your project folder started. Everything else was added on top of that same folder, week after week.", "2. Module (maa·jool) two (2): your data, and your first page, with src/cnn.py and src/data.py. And that page was the first time your project had a face. Something another person could open and look at.", "3. Module (maa·jool) three (3): a trained (traynd) detector (duh·tek·tr), and your second page, with src/train.py and src/evaluate.py. And that's where you learned (lurnd) that a number alone isn't enough (uh·nuhf). You have to look at where the detector (duh·tek·tr) gets it wrong (rong). And that's also where you learned (lurnd) to measure (meh·zhr) with a test set the model never saw. That split is the base of every honest (aa·nuhst) number.", "4. And Module (maa·jool) four (4): a complete system, with your third (thurd) page, and a README (reed·mee) someone else can follow. You built all of it with Claude (klaad) Code. But the decisions (duh·si·zhnz) were yours: what to detect, with what data, with what threshold (thresh·howld), and what to say about its limits. And if you look back, each module (maa·jool) added a page and a piece (pees) of code, but also a way of thinking. Look at the pixels, look at the data, look at the mistakes (muh·stayks), and think about the other person."]}, {"n": 103, "part": 2, "title": "Questions to Keep", "paras": ["More than the code, I want you to keep these four (4) questions. They'll help you with any model.", "1. First: what is this result compared against? A result with no comparison says nothing (nuh·thuhng). A detector (duh·tek·tr) with a certain (sur·tn) mAP (em·ay·pee) can be good or bad. It depends on what you compare it with: another run, another model, or what a person would do.", "2. Second: did it learn the object, or something next to the object? Remember the camera (kam·ruh) trap example from Module (maa·jool) two (2)? The model was looking at the logs, not the animal. This question (kwes·chn) will save you more than once. And that question (kwes·chn) applies to any model you use at work. A model that seems to work very well can be looking at something that always (aal·wayz) appears next to the object, instead of the object. The way to know is always (aal·wayz) the same: look at the images (i·muh·juhz), and test with different cases.", "3. Third (thurd): who pays for each mistake? This question (kwes·chn) is the one that decides the threshold (thresh·howld). And it doesn't have a technical (tek·nuh·kl) answer. It depends on the people who use the system. And it isn't always (aal·wayz) the same person. In a pothole (paat·howl) system, a false (fols) alarm is paid by the crew (kroo), with a wasted trip. A missed (mist) pothole (paat·howl) is paid by the driver who hits it.", "4. And fourth: can someone else run it without me? If you can answer these four (4) questions about any model, for vision (vi·zhn) or anything else, you've learned (lurnd) the most important (uhm·por·tnt) thing in this course. That's this week's question (kwes·chn). And it's the one people forget most when they work alone. And if the answer is no, you already know what to do: test from nothing (nuh·thuhng), write what's missing (mi·suhng), and ask someone to try it."]}, {"n": 104, "part": 2, "title": "Where to Go From Here", "paras": ["And after the course? Three (3) paths.", "1. First, keep your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee). It's work you can show. Before you make it public, check that it has no keys and no personal data. And a repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee) with a good README (reed·mee), and an honest (aa·nuhst) section on what doesn't work, says a lot about you. It shows you know how to build something another person can use. And if you'll (yool) show it in an interview, practise (prak·tuhs) explaining its limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz). It's the part that impresses most, because (buh·kuhz) it shows you understand what you built.", "2. Second, add your extension (uhk·sten·shn): the one you'll (yool) write in Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point two (4.2). And if you like your extension (uhk·sten·shn), build it after the course. You already have everything you need: the structure, the tests, and the way of working with Claude (klaad). And if you build it, write in the README (reed·mee) what changed (chaynjd) and how you checked it. That way your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee) tells the whole story: the system, its limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz), and what you did to improve it.", "3. And third (thurd), try another question (kwes·chn), like segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn) or tracking, with the same method we used in this course. And you don't need to start from zero. The project structure, the tests, the hook and the README (reed·mee) work the same way. Only the data, the model and the results page change. For example, segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn) is a natural step from what you did. Ultralytics (uhl·truh·li·tiks) has segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn) models from the same family, and the project structure is almost (aal·mowst) the same. What changes most is the labeling."]}, {"n": 105, "part": 2, "title": "Before You Make Your Repository Public", "paras": ["I told you to keep your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), because (buh·kuhz) it's work you can show. But before you make it public, or put it on your resume, check five (5) things.", "1. First, no keys or passwords. Search the whole project for words like key, token or password. An API key uploaded by mistake can be used within minutes. And if you find a key you already uploaded, deleting it from the file isn't enough (uh·nuhf). You have to change it in the service, because (buh·kuhz) the old one is already in the history (his·tr·ee).", "2. Second, no .env file in the history (his·tr·ee). And I mean in the history (his·tr·ee), not just in the folder. If you ever uploaded it and deleted it later, it's still (stil) in the earlier commits.", "3. Third (thurd), nothing (nuh·thuhng) personal in the data or the results. Photos (fow·towz) of people, names, the location of your home. If they're in the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee), anyone can see them. And if your dataset (day·tuh·set) comes from a public source, check its license. Some allow sharing the photos (fow·towz) and some don't. The README (reed·mee) can say where to download (daun·lowd) it, instead of including the photos (fow·towz).", "4. Fourth, no big files. The hook already blocked them, but check anyway, in case something got in before the hook existed. And if something big got into the history (his·tr·ee), ask for help before you try to remove it. There are ways to do it, but a mistake there can damage (da·muhj) the repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee).", "5. And fifth, a README (reed·mee) that says what the project is, and what doesn't work. Someone who finds (faindz) it on GitHub (git·huhb), or someone interviewing you for a job, will read the README (reed·mee) first. An honest (aa·nuhst) README (reed·mee), with its limitations (li·muh·tay·shnz), says a lot about how you work. More than the code."]}, {"n": 106, "part": 2, "title": "Choosing Your One Extension", "paras": ["Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point two (4.2) asks you to write one extension (uhk·sten·shn), just one, that would make your system really useful. You don't need to program it. You need to explain it well. Here are four (4) examples for the pothole (paat·howl) system, so you can see the kind of idea.", "1. First, size: small, medium or large. It's the answer to the question (kwes·chn) I asked Claude (klaad). With that, the crew (kroo) knows which truck, and how much material, to bring. Second, a map (em·ay·pee) with the location of each photo, so the city sees where the damage (da·muhj) is concentrated. Third (thurd), testing the system with night or rain photos (fow·towz), to turn a guessed limitation (li·muh·tay·shn) into a measured one. And fourth, using the exported model in the app, so it answers faster on a cheap computer (kuhm·pyoo·dr). Each extension (uhk·sten·shn) starts with what the user gains, not with the technique. And it doesn't have to be one of these four (4). The best one comes from your own project. Think about the person who would use your system, and about what was missing (mi·suhng) when your classmate (klas·mayt) tried it.", "2. One extension (uhk·sten·shn), explained (uhk·splaynd) well, is worth (wurth) more than three (3) that are only started. And explained (uhk·splaynd) well means three things: what it is, who it's for, and what it would need. For example, for size, it would need segmentation (seg·muhn·tay·shn) labels (lay·buhlz) and a calibrated (ka·luh·bray·duhd) camera (kam·ruh), as Claude (klaad) said. If you can say that about your extension (uhk·sten·shn), you're already thinking like someone who designs a system, not just someone who trains a model. And if you don't know what it would need, ask Claude (klaad) the way I did, asking it not to write code. But read its answer carefully (kehr·fuh·lee), and keep only what you understand and can explain."]}, {"n": 107, "part": 2, "title": "Assignment 4.2: A Video of About Three Minutes", "paras": ["And the last assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) of the course: Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point two (4.2), a video of about three (3) minutes.", "1. Six (6) steps. Plan your three (3) minutes. Record the system running, not slides. Say out loud (laud) what it gets wrong (rong). Share (shehr) it with a classmate (klas·mayt) first. Write the one extension (uhk·sten·shn) that would make your system really useful. And publish the link, checked. For the extension (uhk·sten·shn), think about your user, not the model. What would the person using your system need, for it to be really useful? And remember the order. First the plan, then a test recording, then your classmate (klas·mayt), and at the end the version you submit. If you leave it all for the last day, there's no time for the classmate (klas·mayt) part, and that part counts in the grade. And for the extension (uhk·sten·shn), one page is enough (uh·nuhf). You don't need a long document. What you need is for it to be clear what you propose, and why.", "2. And remember: the end matters (ma·drz) most. A real mistake, shown, with your reason. And sharing it with a classmate (klas·mayt) first isn't a formality. It's the only way to know whether (weh·thr) your video makes sense without you next to it, explaining. And if the mistake you show is the same one you wrote in your section on what doesn't work, even better. That way the video and the README (reed·mee) tell the same story, and it shows you know your system. And practise (prak·tuhs) the ending more than the rest. It's the part that counts most, and the one that shows most when you improvise."]}, {"n": 108, "part": 2, "title": "Assignment 4.2: What to Submit", "paras": ["And what do you submit?", "1. As online text, the link to your video, checked in a private window. And a PDF of about one (1) page: where your classmate (klas·mayt) hesitated (heh·zuh·tay·duhd), what you fixed, and your one extension (uhk·sten·shn). And once more: don't upload the video to Moodle (moo·dl). Only the link, and the one-page PDF. In the PDF, be specific (spuh·si·fik). Don't write my classmate (klas·mayt) said it was fine. Write where they hesitated (heh·zuh·tay·duhd), what they understood (uhn·dr·stud) differently, and what you changed (chaynjd) because (buh·kuhz) of it. And the file name, as always (aal·wayz), with your last name and first name. It looks like a detail, but when I review the submissions (suhb·mi·shnz), it helps me not to mix up anyone's work.", "2. Here's the rubric (roo·brik). What counts most is showing the three (3) phases in order. And a real mistake, with your reason, counts as much as showing the system running. And the length. About three (3) minutes. If your video runs much longer, it's almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) because (buh·kuhz) you're explaining the code. Cut that, and show what the system does. And the classmate (klas·mayt) part, the fix and the extension (uhk·sten·shn) count together. Don't leave any of the three (3) blank, even if it's just one sentence."]}, {"n": 109, "part": 2, "title": "Dates and Help", "paras": ["This week's dates.", "1. In the forum: your README (reed·mee) on Wednesday (wenz·day), your reply to a classmate (klas·mayt) on Thursday (thurz·day), your video on Friday, and your reply to a video on Saturday (sa·tr·day). And Assignments four point one (4.1) and four point two (4.2) close on Sunday (suhn·day), November (now·vem·br) twenty-second (22). They're the final assignments of the course. And remember: the forum only works if everyone meets their (dhehr) dates. If someone posts late, their (dhehr) classmate (klas·mayt) has nothing (nuh·thuhng) to review. I recommend you don't wait until (uhn·tel) Sunday (suhn·day). If you finish Assignment (uh·sain·muhnt) four point one (4.1) before Wednesday (wenz·day), you have the rest of the week for the video, which is the part that takes the most time when you do it calmly. And Friday is the date to post the video in the forum, not the final deadline. That way your classmate (klas·mayt) has time to watch it on Saturday (sa·tr·day), and you have time to fix something before Sunday (suhn·day).", "2. And if you get stuck (stuhk) with anything, send me an email with a screenshot (skreen·shaat) of the full error, what you were doing, and whether (weh·thr) you're on Windows, Mac or Linux. I'll (ail) be happy to help. The important (uhm·por·tnt) thing is that you stay in touch. And don't wait for the problem to get big before you write to me. A small error, sent early, is almost (aal·mowst) always (aal·wayz) solved in one email."]}, {"n": 110, "part": 2, "title": "Thank You", "paras": ["And with this, we close the course.", "1. Thank you very much, all of you, for your work and your participation (paar·ti·suh·pay·shn) over these four (4) weeks. It was a pleasure (pleh·zhr) to build this with you. Keep your repository (ruh·paa·zuh·tor·ee): it's work you can show. I wish you a lot of success, and I'll (ail) see you in the submissions (suhb·mi·shnz). And remember. The most important (uhm·por·tnt) thing you take with you isn't the pothole (paat·howl) detector (duh·tek·tr), or the code. It's a way of working. Ask a clear question (kwes·chn), look at the data, measure (meh·zhr) honestly, and say what doesn't work. That will help you in any project you do. It was an intense month. We started by writing a filter by hand, number by number, and we ended with a complete system another person can clone, install, and use. That's a lot of work, and you did it. And one last thing. If at some point you want to show me what you did after the course, write to me. I'll (ail) be very happy to see it."]}];
